ood Handles</b><br> <EOL>The short black polished handles are made of hardwood and are lacquer finished for long life. Only the best brush handles are dipped. They are made by lowering the handles first in primer, then twice in colored lacquer and finally in varnish -- a total of four coats -- for a lustrous durable finish.<br><br> <EOL> <EOL><b>More About Kolinsky Hair</b><br> <EOL>When you see the term ""Kolinsky Sable"" it is not really from sable at all, but comes from the tail of a species that is a member of the weasel family (Mustela) found in Siberia and northeastern China. The best Kolinsky brushes are made with hair from the tail of the male animal's winter coat. Although Kolinskies are also found in northern China and northern Japan, the best hair is from animals in the northern-most parts of Siberia. Kolinskies must be trapped in the wild as opposed to raised on fur farms as mink are today. Kolinsky hair is generally conceded to be the best material for oil and watercolor painting brushes due to its strength, spring and ability to retain its shape (called ""snap""). It holds a very fine point or edge. This is considered a professional grade of hair, and if properly cared for, Kolinsky brushes will last for many years.",,0,0,N,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8459,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments,,32.670,,Monday 01 November 2010 05:08:42 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,280,,14.960,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
