54081,NAP-950-OMP60,ariel2,"Old Master Paper: Palliser (Tan), 18"" x 24"", Made in USA by Natural Pigments","Old Master Paper: Palliser (Tan), 18"" x 24"" by Natural Pigments",0.200,5.500,5.500,,"* Old Master Paper is made from linen and cotton rags like the drawing papers of the 17th and 18th centuries.<br /> <EOL>* Lightweight and strong they are excellent for calligraphy and drawing with charcoal, chalk and sanguine.<br /> <EOL>* They print well in lithography, letterpress, etching and even ink-jet.<br /> <EOL>* It has been used for historic wallpaper and book restorations.",,0,1,N,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8576,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Drawing Tools and Supplies/Old Master Papers,,11.200,,Wednesday 10 November 2010 07:49:33 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,5.500,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
54076,NAP-950-OMP58,ariel2,"Old Master Paper: Rideu (Ivory), 18"" x 24"", Made in USA by Natural Pigments","Old Master Paper: Rideu (Ivory), 18"" x 24"" by Natural Pigments",0.200,5.500,5.500,,"* Old Master Paper Rideu (Ivory): 18"" x 24""<br /> <EOL>* Old Master Paper is made from linen and cotton rags like the drawing papers of the 17th and 18th centuries.<br /> <EOL>* Lightweight and strong they are excellent for calligraphy and drawing with charcoal, chalk and sanguine.<br /> <EOL>* They print well in lithography, letterpress, etching and even ink-jet.<br /> <EOL>* It has been used for historic wallpaper and book restorations.",,0,0,N,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8576,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Drawing Tools and Supplies/Old Master Papers,,7.780,,Wednesday 10 November 2010 07:29:57 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,5.500,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
54082,NAP-950-OMP62,ariel2,"Old Master Paper: Riviere Rouge (Ocher), 18"" x 24"", Made in USA by Natural Pigments","Old Master Paper: Riviere Rouge (Ocher), 18"" x 24"" by Natural Pigments",0.200,5.500,5.500,,"* Old Master Paper is made from linen and cotton rags like the drawing papers of the 17th and 18th centuries.<br /> <EOL>* Lightweight and strong they are excellent for calligraphy and drawing with charcoal, chalk and sanguine.<br /> <EOL>* They print well in lithography, letterpress, etching and even ink-jet.<br /> <EOL>* It has been used for historic wallpaper and book restorations.",,0,1,N,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8576,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Drawing Tools and Supplies/Old Master Papers,,7.780,,Wednesday 10 November 2010 07:56:49 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,5.500,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
55331,NAP-530-43008,bhavesh,Oleogel 237 ml (8 fl oz) Can: Made in USA by Natural Pigments,Oleogel 237 ml (8 fl oz) Can: Made in USA by Natural Pigments,0.500,25.350,25.350,,"Oleogel 237 ml (8 fl oz) Can<BR><br> <EOL><b>About Oleogel </b><Br> <EOL>Oleogel is a thixotropic painting medium made with linseed oil and pyrogenic silica. Oleogel is a clear pale amber gel that adds transparency and thixotropic body to oil, resin-oil or alkyd paint. Add directly to your paint to give it transparency without thinning its consistency. Add pigments or extenders to thicken it for creating impasto effects that do not sink in. <EOL><bR><BR> <EOL>Oleogel does not contain driers, so it is safe to use in oil painting without worry of cracking. When mixed with colors, Oleogel does not slow the drying time. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL> <EOL> <EOL><B>Oleogel adds transparency to whites without losing the body of the paint</b><bR> <EOL>Oleogel in the center, Flake White on the left and a mixture of Oleogel and Flake White on the right. The Flake White is stiff, plastic and short, but when mixed with Oleogel the mixture is semi-transparent, soft, yet sculptural.<BR><br> <EOL> <EOL><b>Oleogel increases transparency without flowout and leveling</b><BR> <EOL>Rublev Colours French Raw Sienna at left and a mixture of Oleogel and Raw Sienna on the right. Below each is a brush out of the above examples. Rublev Colours French Raw Sienna is long, yet holds strokes well. When Oleogel is added to Raw Sienna it increases transparency without causing the flowout and leveling of brushstrokes. <EOL><bR><BR> <EOL><b>Directions</b><BR> <EOL>Mix directly into your paint right on the palette. <EOL><br><bR> <EOL>To make your own paint with Oleogel, add directly to pigment powder until a stiff paste is obtained (almost the consistency of putty). Grind the paste with a muller on a flat surface before storing in collapsible tube. Some of its thixotropic property will be temporarily lost when grinding Oleogel with a muller, but should be restored when allowed to stand.",,999995,21,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8732,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Vehicles & Mediums/Oleoresgels,,22.850,,Wednesday 17 November 2010 10:16:18 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,19.010,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
55313,NAP-530-43002,bhavesh,Oleogel 50 ml Tube: Made in USA by Natural Pigments,Oleogel 50 ml Tube: Made in USA by Natural Pigments,0.100,9.950,6.710,,"Oleogel 50 ml  Tube<BR><br> <EOL><b>About Oleogel </b><Br> <EOL>Oleogel is a thixotropic painting medium made with linseed oil and pyrogenic silica. Oleogel is a clear pale amber gel that adds transparency and thixotropic body to oil, resin-oil or alkyd paint. Add directly to your paint to give it transparency without thinning its consistency. Add pigments or extenders to thicken it for creating impasto effects that do not sink in. <EOL><bR><BR> <EOL>Oleogel does not contain driers, so it is safe to use in oil painting without worry of cracking. When mixed with colors, Oleogel does not slow the drying time. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL> <EOL> <EOL><B>Oleogel adds transparency to whites without losing the body of the paint</b><bR> <EOL>Oleogel in the center, Flake White on the left and a mixture of Oleogel and Flake White on the right. The Flake White is stiff, plastic and short, but when mixed with Oleogel the mixture is semi-transparent, soft, yet sculptural.<BR><br> <EOL> <EOL><b>Directions</b><BR> <EOL>Mix directly into your paint right on the palette. <EOL><br><bR> <EOL>To make your own paint with Oleogel, add directly to pigment powder until a stiff paste is obtained (almost the consistency of putty). Grind the paste with a muller on a flat surface before storing in collapsible tube. Some of its thixotropic property will be temporarily lost when grinding Oleogel with a muller, but should be restored when allowed to stand.",,999969,22,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,57404,Painting and Painting Accessories/Oil Painting/Oil Painting Mediums/Oil Painting Blended Mediums,,10.140,,Wednesday 17 November 2010 09:13:09 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,7.460,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
55309,NAP-530-42004,ariel2,Oleoresgel 4 oz Can: Made in USA by Natural Pigments,Oleoresgel 4 oz Can: Made in USA by Natural Pigments,0.250,16.500,16.500,,"Oleoresgel 4 oz Can<BR><br> <EOL><b>About Wilson's Medium</b><Br> <EOL>Oleoresgel is a thixotropic gel painting medium made with bodied linseed oil, alkyd resin and pyrogenic silica. Oleoresgel is a clear pale amber gel that adds transparency and thixotropic body to oil, resin-oil or alkyd paint. Add directly to your paint to give it transparency without thinning its consistency. Add pigments or extenders to thicken it for creating impasto effects that do not sink in. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL>Oleoresgel does not contain driers, so it is safe to use in oil painting without worry of cracking. When mixed with colors, Oleoresgel does not slow the drying time. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL><b>Oleoresgel adds transparency to whites without losing the body of the paint</b><BR> <EOL>Oleoresgel in the center, Lead White on the left and a mixture of Oleoresgel and Lead White on the right. The Lead White is stiff, plastic and short, but when mixed with Oleoresgel the mixture is semi-transparent, soft, yet sculptural. <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL><b>Oleoresgel increases transparency without flowout and leveling</B><BR> <EOL>Rublev Colours French Raw Sienna at left and a mixture of Oleoresgel and Raw Sienna on the right. Below each is a brush out of the above examples. Rublev Colours French Raw Sienna is long, yet holds strokes well. When Oleoresgel is added to Raw Sienna it increases transparency without causing the flowout and leveling of brushstrokes. <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL><b>Directions</b><BR> <EOL>Mix directly into your paint right on the palette. <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL>To make your own paint with Oleoresgel, add directly to pigment powder until a stiff paste is obtained (almost the consistency of putty). Grind the paste with a muller on a flat surface before storing in collapsible tube. Some of its thixotropic property will be temporarily lost when grinding Oleoresgel with a muller, but should be restored when allowed to stand.",,0,0,N,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8459,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments,,18.740,,Wednesday 17 November 2010 09:06:19 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,8.580,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
105418,NAP-530-42002,bhavesh,Oleoresgel 50 ml Tube: Made in USA by Natural Pigments,Oleoresgel 50 ml Tube: Made in USA by Natural Pigments,0.100,9.950,9.950,,"Oleoresgel 50 ml Tube<BR><br> <EOL><b>About Oleogel </b><Br> <EOL>Oleoresgel is a thixotropic painting medium made with linseed oil and pyrogenic silica. Oleoresgel is a clear pale amber gel that adds transparency and thixotropic body to oil, resin-oil or alkyd paint. Add directly to your paint to give it transparency without thinning its consistency. Add pigments or extenders to thicken it for creating impasto effects that do not sink in. <EOL><bR><BR> <EOL>Oleoresgel does not contain driers, so it is safe to use in oil painting without worry of cracking. When mixed with colors, Oleoresgel does not slow the drying time. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL> <EOL><B>Oleoresgel adds transparency to whites without losing the body of the paint</b><bR> <EOL>Oleogel in the center, Flake White on the left and a mixture of Oleogel and Flake White on the right. The Flake White is stiff, plastic and short, but when mixed with Oleogel the mixture is semi-transparent, soft, yet sculptural.<BR><br> <EOL> <EOL><b>Directions</b><BR> <EOL>Mix directly into your paint right on the palette. <EOL><br><bR> <EOL>To make your own paint with Oleoresgel, add directly to pigment powder until a stiff paste is obtained (almost the consistency of putty). Grind the paste with a muller on a flat surface before storing in collapsible tube. Some of its thixotropic property will be temporarily lost when grinding Oleogel with a muller, but should be restored when allowed to stand.",,99999,2,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,57404,Painting and Painting Accessories/Oil Painting/Oil Painting Mediums/Oil Painting Blended Mediums,,10.140,,Tuesday 08 November 2011 05:21:32 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,7.460,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
55064,NAP-510-32SFO1K,bhavesh,Orange Dewaxed Shellac Flakes 1 kg (2.2 lb) Bag: Made in USA by Natural Pigments,Orange Dewaxed Shellac Flakes 1 kg (2.2 lb) Bag by Natural Pigments,2.200,78.500,78.500,,"Orange Dewaxed Shellac Flakes 1 kg (2.2 lb) Bag<BR><br> <EOL><b>Specifications</b><BR> <EOL>* Chemical Name: dihydroxyficocerylic acid (essential ingredient)<br> <EOL>* Chemical Formula: CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>(CHOH)(CH<sub>2</sub>)<sub>7</sub>CHOH-COOH<bR> <EOL>* Density: 1.110–1.196<br> <EOL>* Hardness: 2.0–2.5<br> <EOL>* Refractive Index: n=1.520<br><br> <EOL> <EOL><b>About Dewaxed Decolorized Shellac</b><br> <EOL>Dewaxed Decolorized Shellac is produced from carefully selected raw materials by the solvent extraction system using activated carbon. With the solvent process wax and impurities are removed by dissolving the seedlac in a solvent, usually, chilled industrial alcohol. The solution is then filtered through fine cloth and the alcohol is recovered by boiling the solution. The dissolved shellac is then stretched to the required thickness on a roller. The product obtained is called dewaxed shellac. Highly sophisticated grades of dewaxed and decolorized shellac are further processed using activated carbon as the decolorizing agent. <EOL><bR><br> <EOL><b>Uses</b><BR> <EOL>Orange Shellac is an excellent quick-drying finish. The flake form allows fresh quality shellac to be prepared and avoids waste. Use shellac as a sealant on wood after cleaning and prior to painting or finishing, on gilding before painting or varnishing, on grounds to reduce its absorbency, and on paint to isolate layers. <EOL><bR><BR> <EOL><b>Origin and History</b><BR> <EOL>The source of shellac from lac has been known since earliest recorded times. Atharva Veda, estimated to be several thousand years old, clearly states that both lac as well as lac dye served as medicine. It was used in China as medicine as well, long before it was used in art. The original cultivation of lac for resin was for the dye that gives the resin its characteristic color. The use of the lac dye can be traced back to 250 C.E. when it was mentioned by Claudius Aelianus, a Roman writer on natural history. The use of shellac as a protective coating appears as early as 1590 in a work by an English writer who was sent to India to observe the country and its people. <EOL><bR><BR> <EOL><b>Source</b><BR> <EOL>Shellac is an animal product secreted by the Coccus lacca, a scale insect that feeds on certain trees in India and southern Asia. Soon after the scale insect is hatched, it leaves its birthplace and begins to wander about, looking for a likely place to settle down. It finds this place upon a stem or a leaf or on the outside of a fruit. It inserts its sharp little beak in the plant tissue and then settles down for a lifetime of nourishment at this spot. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL>After feeding, the insect produces a gummy substance through its pores. It is soon covered by its secretion that in certain species looks like wax, in others like cotton, in still others like powder. The growing insect sheds one skin after another; these skins form a rounded scale, held in place by the secreted substance, which hardens into a protective covering called lac. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL>This lac is collected and then it is crushed, washed and dried. After further treatment, it is skillfully drawn into thin sheets of finished shellac. The level of refinement, the timing of harvest, and source of the lac, determine the color and specifications that makes the various grades of shellac. <EOL><Br><BR> <EOL> <EOL><b>Preparation</b><BR> <EOL><b>Ingredient 	Parts by weight</b><BR> <EOL>* Shellac: 1<bR> <EOL>* Methyl alcohol: 5<Br><BR> <EOL>Denatured alcohol can be substituted for methyl alcohol (denatured alcohol is ethyl alcohol with a small amount of methyl alcohol to prevent it from being ingested), but shellac dissolves better in methyl alcohol.<br><BR> <EOL> <EOL>Combine the ingredients in a dark plastic or glass container with a tightly fitting lid. Soak the shellac flakes in about half of the total alcohol to be used for 24 hours or longer (cool room temperatures slow the process). If you are using button shellac pulverize it to hasten dissolving. Stir occasionally and when dissolved add the balance of the alcohol. <EOL><Br><BR> <EOL>After the shellac is fully dissolved, it should be strained through a fine mesh cheese cloth to remove any impurities. Shellac is made from the lac bug and a few bits of bug carcass are often left particularly in Seed Lac. Store in a cool and dark place. <EOL><br><Br> <EOL>Before the liquid shellac is used, it should be shaken or stirred thoroughly and allowed to stand for a few hours. Thinned with ten times its volume of alcohol, this solution serves well as a fixative. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL> <EOL><b>Application</b><BR> <EOL>When applying as an undercoat prior to other paint or finishes use dewaxed shellac. Dip a natural bristle brush about half way into the shellac and gently remove excess shellac against the side of the container; this gives a reasonably filled brush for full strokes without incorporating air in the shellac. Apply the shellac in long strokes. Shellac should be sanded between coats. Allow each coat to dry thoroughly. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL>If the shellac is dry, sanding will produce a fine powder on the surface. If the shellac is not dry it will be somewhat tacky to sand and the paper will clog. After sanding, wipe the surface thoroughly with a tack cloth and recoat. Depending upon temperature and humidity conditions, allow from two to four hours drying for each coat. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL>Test all mixed shellac for drying if several months old. If the surface stays tacky after 8 hours and does not sand freely without gumming, the shellac is old and will not dry and must be discarded. <EOL><Br><BR> <EOL><b>Toxicity</b><BR> <EOL>Shellac is not considered to be toxic, but care should be used in handling the ground product to avoid inhaling the dust.",,0,4,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8730,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Vehicles & Mediums/Shellac Flakes,,70.120,,Tuesday 16 November 2010 07:26:04 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,58.880,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
55059,NAP-510-32SFO10,bhavesh,Orange Dewaxed Shellac Flakes 100 g (3.5 oz) Bag: Made in USA by Natural Pigments,Orange Dewaxed Shellac Flakes 100 g (3.5 oz) Bag by Natural Pigments,0.220,15.500,15.500,,"Orange Dewaxed Shellac Flakes 100 g (3.5 oz) Bag<BR><br> <EOL><b>Specifications</b><BR> <EOL>* Chemical Name: dihydroxyficocerylic acid (essential ingredient)<br> <EOL>* Chemical Formula: CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>(CHOH)(CH<sub>2</sub>)<sub>7</sub>CHOH-COOH<bR> <EOL>* Density: 1.110–1.196<br> <EOL>* Hardness: 2.0–2.5<br> <EOL>* Refractive Index: n=1.520<br><br> <EOL> <EOL><b>About Dewaxed Decolorized Shellac</b><br> <EOL>Dewaxed Orange Shellac is produced from carefully selected raw materials by the solvent extraction system. With the solvent process wax and impurities are removed by dissolving the seedlac in a solvent, usually, chilled industrial alcohol. The solution is then filtered through fine cloth and the alcohol is recovered by boiling the solution. The dissolved shellac is then stretched to the required thickness on a roller. The product obtained is called dewaxed shellac. Decolorized grades of dewaxed shellac are further processed using activated carbon as a decolorizing agent. <EOL><br /><br /> <EOL>Orange shellac is the most popular shellac used in antique restoration and in reproductions of antiques. May also be used as a sealer coat over dark woods to seal in oils and natural resins. Orange shellac is also used to seal in wood knots. Orange shellac gives a warm tint. Dewaxed orange makes orange brown varnish. It's easier to build coats by brushing without streaking from dewaxed dark or garnet grades. <EOL><bR><br> <EOL><b>Uses</b><BR> <EOL>Orange Shellac is an excellent quick-drying finish. The flake form allows fresh quality shellac to be prepared and avoids waste. Use shellac as a sealant on wood after cleaning and prior to painting or finishing, on gilding before painting or varnishing, on grounds to reduce its absorbency, and on paint to isolate layers. <EOL><bR><BR> <EOL><b>Origin and History</b><BR> <EOL>The source of shellac from lac has been known since earliest recorded times. Atharva Veda, estimated to be several thousand years old, clearly states that both lac as well as lac dye served as medicine. It was used in China as medicine as well, long before it was used in art. The original cultivation of lac for resin was for the dye that gives the resin its characteristic color. The use of the lac dye can be traced back to 250 C.E. when it was mentioned by Claudius Aelianus, a Roman writer on natural history. The use of shellac as a protective coating appears as early as 1590 in a work by an English writer who was sent to India to observe the country and its people. <EOL><bR><BR> <EOL><b>Source</b><BR> <EOL>Shellac is an animal product secreted by the Coccus lacca, a scale insect that feeds on certain trees in India and southern Asia. Soon after the scale insect is hatched, it leaves its birthplace and begins to wander about, looking for a likely place to settle down. It finds this place upon a stem or a leaf or on the outside of a fruit. It inserts its sharp little beak in the plant tissue and then settles down for a lifetime of nourishment at this spot. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL>After feeding, the insect produces a gummy substance through its pores. It is soon covered by its secretion that in certain species looks like wax, in others like cotton, in still others like powder. The growing insect sheds one skin after another; these skins form a rounded scale, held in place by the secreted substance, which hardens into a protective covering called lac. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL>This lac is collected and then it is crushed, washed and dried. After further treatment, it is skillfully drawn into thin sheets of finished shellac. The level of refinement, the timing of harvest, and source of the lac, determine the color and specifications that makes the various grades of shellac. <EOL><Br><BR> <EOL> <EOL><b>Preparation</b><BR> <EOL><b>Ingredient 	Parts by weight</b><BR> <EOL>* Shellac: 1<bR> <EOL>* Methyl alcohol: 5<Br><BR> <EOL>Denatured alcohol can be substituted for methyl alcohol (denatured alcohol is ethyl alcohol with a small amount of methyl alcohol to prevent it from being ingested), but shellac dissolves better in methyl alcohol.<br><BR> <EOL> <EOL>Combine the ingredients in a dark plastic or glass container with a tightly fitting lid. Soak the shellac flakes in about half of the total alcohol to be used for 24 hours or longer (cool room temperatures slow the process). If you are using button shellac pulverize it to hasten dissolving. Stir occasionally and when dissolved add the balance of the alcohol. <EOL><Br><BR> <EOL>After the shellac is fully dissolved, it should be strained through a fine mesh cheese cloth to remove any impurities. Shellac is made from the lac bug and a few bits of bug carcass are often left particularly in Seed Lac. Store in a cool and dark place. <EOL><br><Br> <EOL>Before the liquid shellac is used, it should be shaken or stirred thoroughly and allowed to stand for a few hours. Thinned with ten times its volume of alcohol, this solution serves well as a fixative. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL> <EOL><b>Application</b><BR> <EOL>When applying as an undercoat prior to other paint or finishes use dewaxed shellac. Dip a natural bristle brush about half way into the shellac and gently remove excess shellac against the side of the container; this gives a reasonably filled brush for full strokes without incorporating air in the shellac. Apply the shellac in long strokes. Shellac should be sanded between coats. Allow each coat to dry thoroughly. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL>If the shellac is dry, sanding will produce a fine powder on the surface. If the shellac is not dry it will be somewhat tacky to sand and the paper will clog. After sanding, wipe the surface thoroughly with a tack cloth and recoat. Depending upon temperature and humidity conditions, allow from two to four hours drying for each coat. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL>Test all mixed shellac for drying if several months old. If the surface stays tacky after 8 hours and does not sand freely without gumming, the shellac is old and will not dry and must be discarded. <EOL><Br><BR> <EOL><b>Toxicity</b><BR> <EOL>Shellac is not considered to be toxic, but care should be used in handling the ground product to avoid inhaling the dust.",,999891,7,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8730,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Vehicles & Mediums/Shellac Flakes,,15.680,,Tuesday 16 November 2010 07:21:08 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,11.630,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
55068,NAP-510-32SFO50,bhavesh,Orange Dewaxed Shellac Flakes 500 g (1.1 lb) Bag: Made in USA by Natural Pigments,Orange Dewaxed Shellac Flakes 500 g (1.1 lb) Bag by Natural Pigments,1.100,42.500,42.500,,"Orange Dewaxed Shellac Flakes 500 g (1.1 lb) Bag<BR><br> <EOL><b>Specifications</b><BR> <EOL>* Chemical Name: dihydroxyficocerylic acid (essential ingredient)<br> <EOL>* Chemical Formula: CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>(CHOH)(CH<sub>2</sub>)<sub>7</sub>CHOH-COOH<bR> <EOL>* Density: 1.110–1.196<br> <EOL>* Hardness: 2.0–2.5<br> <EOL>* Refractive Index: n=1.520<br><br> <EOL> <EOL><b>About Dewaxed Orange Shellac</b><br> <EOL>Dewaxed Orange Shellac is produced from carefully selected raw materials by the solvent extraction system using activated carbon. With the solvent process wax and impurities are removed by dissolving the seedlac in a solvent, usually, chilled industrial alcohol. The solution is then filtered through fine cloth and the alcohol is recovered by boiling the solution. The dissolved shellac is then stretched to the required thickness on a roller. The product obtained is called dewaxed shellac. Highly sophisticated grades of dewaxed and decolorized shellac are further processed using activated carbon as the decolorizing agent. <EOL><bR><br> <EOL><b>Uses</b><BR> <EOL>Shellac is an excellent quick-drying finish. The flake form allows fresh quality shellac to be prepared and avoids waste. Use shellac as a sealant on wood after cleaning and prior to painting or finishing, on gilding before painting or varnishing, on grounds to reduce its absorbency, and on paint to isolate layers. <EOL><bR><BR> <EOL><b>Origin and History</b><BR> <EOL>The source of shellac from lac has been known since earliest recorded times. Atharva Veda, estimated to be several thousand years old, clearly states that both lac as well as lac dye served as medicine. It was used in China as medicine as well, long before it was used in art. The original cultivation of lac for resin was for the dye that gives the resin its characteristic color. The use of the lac dye can be traced back to 250 C.E. when it was mentioned by Claudius Aelianus, a Roman writer on natural history. The use of shellac as a protective coating appears as early as 1590 in a work by an English writer who was sent to India to observe the country and its people. <EOL><bR><BR> <EOL><b>Source</b><BR> <EOL>Shellac is an animal product secreted by the Coccus lacca, a scale insect that feeds on certain trees in India and southern Asia. Soon after the scale insect is hatched, it leaves its birthplace and begins to wander about, looking for a likely place to settle down. It finds this place upon a stem or a leaf or on the outside of a fruit. It inserts its sharp little beak in the plant tissue and then settles down for a lifetime of nourishment at this spot. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL>After feeding, the insect produces a gummy substance through its pores. It is soon covered by its secretion that in certain species looks like wax, in others like cotton, in still others like powder. The growing insect sheds one skin after another; these skins form a rounded scale, held in place by the secreted substance, which hardens into a protective covering called lac. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL>This lac is collected and then it is crushed, washed and dried. After further treatment, it is skillfully drawn into thin sheets of finished shellac. The level of refinement, the timing of harvest, and source of the lac, determine the color and specifications that makes the various grades of shellac. <EOL><Br><BR> <EOL> <EOL><b>Preparation</b><BR> <EOL><b>Ingredient 	Parts by weight</b><BR> <EOL>* Shellac: 1<bR> <EOL>* Methyl alcohol: 5<Br><BR> <EOL>Denatured alcohol can be substituted for methyl alcohol (denatured alcohol is ethyl alcohol with a small amount of methyl alcohol to prevent it from being ingested), but shellac dissolves better in methyl alcohol.<br><BR> <EOL> <EOL>Combine the ingredients in a dark plastic or glass container with a tightly fitting lid. Soak the shellac flakes in about half of the total alcohol to be used for 24 hours or longer (cool room temperatures slow the process). If you are using button shellac pulverize it to hasten dissolving. Stir occasionally and when dissolved add the balance of the alcohol. <EOL><Br><BR> <EOL>After the shellac is fully dissolved, it should be strained through a fine mesh cheese cloth to remove any impurities. Shellac is made from the lac bug and a few bits of bug carcass are often left particularly in Seed Lac. Store in a cool and dark place. <EOL><br><Br> <EOL>Before the liquid shellac is used, it should be shaken or stirred thoroughly and allowed to stand for a few hours. Thinned with ten times its volume of alcohol, this solution serves well as a fixative. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL> <EOL><b>Application</b><BR> <EOL>When applying as an undercoat prior to other paint or finishes use dewaxed shellac. Dip a natural bristle brush about half way into the shellac and gently remove excess shellac against the side of the container; this gives a reasonably filled brush for full strokes without incorporating air in the shellac. Apply the shellac in long strokes. Shellac should be sanded between coats. Allow each coat to dry thoroughly. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL>If the shellac is dry, sanding will produce a fine powder on the surface. If the shellac is not dry it will be somewhat tacky to sand and the paper will clog. After sanding, wipe the surface thoroughly with a tack cloth and recoat. Depending upon temperature and humidity conditions, allow from two to four hours drying for each coat. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL>Test all mixed shellac for drying if several months old. If the surface stays tacky after 8 hours and does not sand freely without gumming, the shellac is old and will not dry and must be discarded. <EOL><Br><BR> <EOL><b>Toxicity</b><BR> <EOL>Shellac is not considered to be toxic, but care should be used in handling the ground product to avoid inhaling the dust.",,0,2,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8730,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Vehicles & Mediums/Shellac Flakes,,38.110,,Tuesday 16 November 2010 07:31:01 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,31.880,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
55040,NAP-510-15OCL50,bhavesh,Organoclay 500 g (1.1 lb) Bag: Made in USA by Natural Pigments,Organoclay 500 g (1.1 lb) Bag: Made in USA by Natural Pigments,1.100,16.950,12.710,,"Organoclay 500 g (1.1 lb) Bag<BR><br> <EOL><b>Specifications</b><BR> <EOL>* Name: Bentone SD-1<bR> <EOL>* Composition: Organic derivative of a bentonite clay<br> <EOL>* Form: Off white, finely divided powder<br> <EOL>* Bulk Density, Loose: 1.47 g/cm3<br> <EOL> <EOL><b>About Organoclay </b><br> <EOL>Organoclay (Bentone SD-1) is is an organic derivative of bentonite clay and used as a rheological additive. Mixes easily with oil and alkyd paints to increase viscosity, prevent pigment settling, and increase crispness of handling. Add 1–4% of total weight of the paint. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL>It provides viscosity increase, reduces sagging of coatings on vertical surfaces and prevents settling of pigments and fillers. <EOL><bR><BR> <EOL><b>How to Use</b><br> <EOL>Organoclay is readily dispersible for non-polar to medium-polarity organic applications. It may be added at any point in the paint making process is using all normal paint-making equipment. This rheological additive can be incorporated in the pigment grind under high shear and under low shear. A chemical activator is not required to disperse the organoclay in conventional solvent containing paints. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL>It can be added to the pigment grind or by mixing rather than grinding. Its does, however, require some minimal shear (minimum tip speed 4 meters per second when a high speed mixer is used). The liquid paint to which it is to be added should well stirred as it is sifted in. Sufficient time for full activation should be allowed. The amount of time required will depend upon the amount of paint, the type and speed of mixer in use and the viscosity and solids of the system. When grinding with the pigment, it is not sensitive to high temperatures and no minimum temperature is required. <EOL>",,999995,2,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,50263,Furniture for Artists/Lamps and Lighting,,17,,Tuesday 16 November 2010 06:09:21 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,12.710,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
58447,NAP-630-PY1042,ariel2,"Painters Pyramid: Blue, Pack of 10","Painters Pyramid: Blue, Pack of 10",0.100,3.950,3.950,,"<B>Painters Pyramid: Blue, Pack of 10</b><BR><BR> <EOL><B>About Painters Pyramid</b><BR> <EOL>Painters pyramids are a durable, versatile tool that elevates an object enabling the user to complete the sides and one surface of a project in one session. They also allow easier access to edges. Supports both small and large projects for less mess, easier set up, reduced clean up, and faster drying. Non-stick material is impermeable to paints and solvents. Rounded tips will not penetrate the objects being supported. Tips make only minimal contact with the object being painted so touch-ups aren’t required. Approximately 2-inches (5 cm) high, the pyramids hold up to 200 lbs. and are lightweight, stackable, re-usable and are easy to store. Use for painting, gluing, applying primer or grounds to panels, faux finishing and much more.",,0,1,N,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8459,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments,,4.670,,Saturday 11 December 2010 10:32:06 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,2.050,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
58445,NAP-630-PY1028,rommel,"Painters Pyramid: Red, Pack of 10","Painters Pyramid: Red, Pack of 10",0.100,6.990,6.990,,"<B>Painters Pyramid: Red, Pack of 10</b><BR><BR> <EOL><B>About Painters Pyramid</b><BR> <EOL>Painters pyramids are a durable, versatile tool that elevates an object enabling the user to complete the sides and one surface of a project in one session. They also allow easier access to edges. Supports both small and large projects for less mess, easier set up, reduced clean up, and faster drying. Non-stick material is impermeable to paints and solvents. Rounded tips will not penetrate the objects being supported. Tips make only minimal contact with the object being painted so touch-ups aren’t required. Approximately 2-inches (5 cm) high, the pyramids hold up to 200 lbs. and are lightweight, stackable, re-usable and are easy to store. Use for painting, gluing, applying primer or grounds to panels, faux finishing and much more.",,1000000,0,N,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8459,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments,,8.270,,Saturday 11 December 2010 10:27:38 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,3.630,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
55185,NAP-510-41OWD08,bhavesh,Pale Drying Linseed Oil 8 oz (237 ml) Bottle: Made in USA by Natural Pigments,Pale Drying Linseed Oil 8 oz (237 ml) Bottle by Natural Pigments,0.500,9.500,9.500,,"Pale Drying Linseed Oil 8 oz (237 ml) Bottle<BR><br> <EOL><b>Specifications</b><BR> <EOL>* Natural Source: Linseed Linum usitatissimum<bR> <EOL>* Acid Value (mg KOH/g), max: 1.1 <br> <EOL>* Color (Gardner): 11<br> <EOL>* Iodine Value: 181.2<br> <EOL>* Viscosity (Gardner @ 25° C): A <br> <EOL>* Appearance: Clear/Transparent at 25° C<br> <EOL>* Specific gravity: 0.928 @ 25° C<br> <EOL>* Weight Per Gallon: 7.742 lbs.<br> <EOL>* Dry Time: 16 hrs. max<bR><br> <EOL> <EOL><b>About Vacuum-Bodied Oil</b><BR> <EOL>Natural Pigments Pale Drying Oil is processed from well-settled, filtered raw linseed oil made from selected North American flax seeds. The oil is heated in closed kettles with the scientific addition of driers. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL>In the past, before soluble liquid driers were developed, metallic driers could only be dissolved and thus made effective by reacting the metal salt or oxide (such as litharge or manganese borate) with the oil at high temperature. It was therefore necessary to heat or ""boil"" the oil, if you wanted an oil that already contained a drier. Our pale drying oil is made by heating raw linseed oil at a low temperature to slightly polymerize it and adding liquid driers. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL>Natural Pigments Pale Drying Oil is fast drying. Add small amounts – up to 30% – to your grinding oil when making oil colors or to other tube oil paint to promote faster drying. Add to painting mediums and oil colors to speed drying without the danger of adding excessive amounts of driers. This is a good substitute for ""black oil"" and other drying oils. <EOL><bR><BR> <EOL>Use turpentine, spike oil or mineral spirits as the solvent for oil painting mediums and for brush and studio clean up. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL>Linseed oil is a yellowish drying oil derived from the dried ripe seeds of the flax plant (Linum usitatissimum, Linaceae).<Br><BR> <EOL> <EOL> <EOL><B>Warning:</b><Br> Rags or paper towels contaminated with vegetable drying oils, particularly those containing iron oxide pigments are susceptible to spontaneous combustion. To prevent unexpected fires, used rags or paper towels contaminated with oil-based materials should be collected in a closeable, air-tight container. Store water-dampened rags or paper towels in a metal container with an air-tight top. Alternately, washing contaminated rags will remove contaminating materials and eliminate risk. <EOL>",,999998,6,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8727,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Vehicles & Mediums/Oils,,9.690,,Tuesday 16 November 2010 10:54:14 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,7.130,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
55175,NAP-510-41OPG08,bhavesh,Pale Grinders Oil 8 oz (237 ml) Bottle: Made in USA by Natural Pigments,Pale Grinders Oil 8 oz (237 ml) Bottle by Natural Pigments,0.500,9.500,9.500,,"Pale Grinders Oil 8 oz (237 ml) Bottle<BR><br> <EOL><b>Specifications</b><BR> <EOL>* Natural Source: Linseed Linum usitatissimum<bR> <EOL>* Acid Value (mg KOH/g), max: 12.6 <br> <EOL>* Color (Gardner), max.: 6 <br> <EOL>* Iodine Value: 176 <br> <EOL>* Viscosity (Gardner-Holdt @ 25° C): A<br> <EOL>* Saponification Value: 162-172<br> <EOL>* Appearance: Clear/Transparent at 25° C<br> <EOL>* Specific gravity: 0.930 @ 25° C<br> <EOL>* Weight Per Gallon: 7.73 lbs.<br><BR> <EOL> <EOL><b>About Grinders Oil</b><BR> <EOL>Refined, bleached and dewaxed linseed oil and linseed oil fatty acids, Pale Grinders is exceptionally light in color and well-suited for grinding colors. Its high acidity gives it excellent pigment wetting properties for making your own oil paint with dry pigments. High acid value oils make it easier to wet pigments difficult to grind, such as lakes and lamp black, and are especially valuable for grinding reactive pigments, such as lead white. <EOL><bR><BR> <EOL>Use turpentine, spike oil or mineral spirits as the solvent for oil painting mediums and for brush and studio clean up. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL>Linseed oil is a yellowish drying oil derived from the dried ripe seeds of the flax plant (Linum usitatissimum, Linaceae). <EOL><bR><BR> <EOL><b>Warning:</b><Br> Rags or paper towels contaminated with vegetable drying oils, particularly those containing iron oxide pigments are susceptible to spontaneous combustion. To prevent unexpected fires, used rags or paper towels contaminated with oil-based materials should be collected in a closeable, air-tight container. Store water-dampened rags or paper towels in a metal container with an air-tight top. Alternately, washing contaminated rags will remove contaminating materials and eliminate risk. <EOL>",,1000000,0,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8727,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Vehicles & Mediums/Oils,,9.690,,Tuesday 16 November 2010 10:15:20 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,7.130,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
58408,NAP-624-PNTR12,bhavesh,Palette with 12 Jars,Palette with 12 Jars,0.500,6.990,6.990,,"<B>Palette with 12 Jars</b><BR><BR> <EOL><B>Large Folding Palette</b><BR> <EOL>White heavy-duty plastic palette, 8 inches long by 6 inches wide inches (20 x 15 cm), with 12 clear plastic jars and airtight fitting caps along two sides and four wells in the center. This palette can be used with all media, but is ideally suited for tempera painters who may store paint from one session to the next in the jars. The clear plastic jars have caps that snap on to form airtight seals for storing paint.",,999999,1,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8932,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Tools,,9.500,,Saturday 11 December 2010 08:57:46 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,6.990,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
53901,NAP-655-APL3,ariel2,"Pencil Lengthener and Holder: Pack of 3, Made in USA by Natural Pigments","Pencil Lengthener and Holder: Pack of 3, by Natural Pigments",1,7.950,7.950,,Pencil Lengthener and Holder: Pack of 3 <EOL><br /><br /> <EOL>Pencil Lengthener and Holder provides balance and control when pencils or charcoal sticks get too short. Saves money by extending the usable life of pencils and other drawing tools. Fits most round and hexagonal standard pencils. Made of anodized aluminum in three colors per package.,,999990,1,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,50572,Drawing and Illustration/Pencil Extenders,,10.720,,Tuesday 09 November 2010 08:59:30 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,7.950,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
54737,NAP-510-15PRL1K,bhavesh,Perlite 1 Kg (2.2 lb) Bag: Made in USA by Natural Pigments,Perlite 1 Kg (2.2 lb) Bag: Made in USA by Natural Pigments,2.200,11.500,11.500,,"Perlite 1 Kg (2.2 lb) Bag<BR><br> <EOL><b>Specifications</b><BR> <EOL><b>Physical Properties</b><br> <EOL>* Mineral: Perlite: Amorphous Silicate<Br> <EOL>* Particle Shape: Semi-Polyhedron, Plate-Like<br> <EOL>* Residue 40 Mesh (%): 2.0<br> <EOL>* Residue 140 Mesh (%): 2.0 - 14.0<br> <EOL>* Bulk Density (lbs/cu ft): 7.0 - 9.0<br> <EOL>* Specific Gravity: 2.2 - 2.4<br> <EOL>* Mohs Hardness: 2.0 - 2.5<br> <EOL>* pH Value (10% slurry): 5.0 - 8.0<br> <EOL>* Color: White<br> <EOL>* Refractive Index: 1.5<br> <EOL>* Moisture Content (max. %): <1.5<br><BR> <EOL><b>Elemental Analysis</b><BR> <EOL>* Silicon (Si): 33.8%<br> <EOL>* Aluminum (Al): 7.2%<br> <EOL>* Potassium (K): 3.5%<br> <EOL>* Sodium (Na): 3.4%<br> <EOL>* Oxygen (O): 47.5%<br> <EOL>* Others: 1.6%<br> <EOL>* Combined Water (H<sub>2</sub>O): 3.0%<br><br> <EOL><b>About Perlite </b><br> <EOL>Perlite is an amorphous volcanic glass that has a relatively high water content, typically formed by the hydration of obsidian. It occurs naturally and has the unusual property of greatly expanding when heated sufficiently. It is an industrial mineral and a commercial product useful for its light weight after processing. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL><b>Properties</b><Br> <EOL>When crude perlite is heated rapidly to temperatures above 870° C (1,600° F), it pops in a manner similar to popcorn creating countless tiny bubbles in the heat softened glassy particles. The combined water vaporizes and escapes, causing the material to expand 7 to 16 times its original volume. Unprocessed perlite has a bulk density around 1100 kg/m3 (1.1 g/cm3), while expanded perlite typically has a bulk density of about 30–150 kg/m3, so it is much lighter in weight per volume. It is these tiny glass-sealed bubbles that account for the amazing light weight and other exceptional physical properties of expanded perlite. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL>The expansion process also creates one of perlite's most distinguishing characteristics: its white color. While the crude perlite rock may range from transparent to light gray to glossy black, the color of expanded perlite ranges from snowy white to grayish white. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL><b>Source</b><BR> <EOL>Our perlite comes from the Nu-Alexite and Socorro perlite deposits located in New Mexico, U.S. The ore is formed by magmatic processes, usually in ""domes,"" that over geologic time periods entrap water into the matrix. The mechanism through which this occurs is not completely understood, however, it is this entrapped water which gives perlite ore it's unique ability to expand. The Socorro deposit has proven reserves of over 10 million tons and is unique in that it is believed to be the deepest known perlite deposit in the world. These characteristics give the processed Socorro ore unmatched quality and consistency. The perlite ore is surface mined and is usually light gray in color. All perlite ore contains low crystalline silica contents in comparison with other industrial minerals. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL><b>Uses</b><BR> <EOL>Due to its low density and relatively low price, many commercial applications for perlite have developed. In the construction and manufacturing fields, it is used in lightweight plasters and mortars, insulation and ceiling tiles. <EOL><bR><BR> <EOL>Perlite is an excellent filter aid. It is used extensively as an alternative to diatomaceous earth. The popularity of perlite usage in this application is growing considerably worldwide. <EOL><bR><BR> <EOL>Although applications of perlite in art are uncommon, everyday it is being used more and more in such applications as plaster, ceramics, foundry casting and paint. <EOL><bR><BR> <EOL><b>Use in Paint</b><Br> <EOL>Perlite can be added to paint and painting mediums, especially water-based, to add bulk. For example, add perlite to calcite mediums to increase the bulk of the medium or paint for thick impastos. It can also be used to prevent settling of pigments and heavy extenders. It can also be used to add texture or build the consistency (thicken) of paint. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL><b>Use in Grounds</B><br> <EOL>A novel use of perlite is in painting grounds. Add an amount to a traditional gesso or chalk ground to increase the bulk and decrease its weight. <EOL> <EOL>",,999997,2,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8678,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Grounds/Perlite & Technical Gelatin Bags,,11.640,,Saturday 13 November 2010 10:52:37 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,8.630,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
54736,NAP-510-15PRL50,bhavesh,Perlite 500 g (1.1 lb) Bag: Made in USA by Natural Pigments,Perlite 500 g (1.1 lb) Bag: Made in USA by Natural Pigments,1.100,7.500,5.630,,"Perlite 500 g (1.1 lb) Bag<BR><br> <EOL><b>Specifications</b><BR> <EOL><b>Physical Properties</b><br> <EOL>* Mineral: Perlite: Amorphous Silicate<Br> <EOL>* Particle Shape: Semi-Polyhedron, Plate-Like<br> <EOL>* Residue 40 Mesh (%): 2.0<br> <EOL>* Residue 140 Mesh (%): 2.0 - 14.0<br> <EOL>* Bulk Density (lbs/cu ft): 7.0 - 9.0<br> <EOL>* Specific Gravity: 2.2 - 2.4<br> <EOL>* Mohs Hardness: 2.0 - 2.5<br> <EOL>* pH Value (10% slurry): 5.0 - 8.0<br> <EOL>* Color: White<br> <EOL>* Refractive Index: 1.5<br> <EOL>* Moisture Content (max. %): <1.5<br><BR> <EOL><b>Elemental Analysis</b><BR> <EOL>* Silicon (Si): 33.8%<br> <EOL>* Aluminum (Al): 7.2%<br> <EOL>* Potassium (K): 3.5%<br> <EOL>* Sodium (Na): 3.4%<br> <EOL>* Oxygen (O): 47.5%<br> <EOL>* Others: 1.6%<br> <EOL>* Combined Water (H<sub>2</sub>O): 3.0%<br><br> <EOL><b>About Perlite </b><br> <EOL>Perlite is an amorphous volcanic glass that has a relatively high water content, typically formed by the hydration of obsidian. It occurs naturally and has the unusual property of greatly expanding when heated sufficiently. It is an industrial mineral and a commercial product useful for its light weight after processing. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL><b>Properties</b><Br> <EOL>When crude perlite is heated rapidly to temperatures above 870° C (1,600° F), it pops in a manner similar to popcorn creating countless tiny bubbles in the heat softened glassy particles. The combined water vaporizes and escapes, causing the material to expand 7 to 16 times its original volume. Unprocessed perlite has a bulk density around 1100 kg/m3 (1.1 g/cm3), while expanded perlite typically has a bulk density of about 30–150 kg/m3, so it is much lighter in weight per volume. It is these tiny glass-sealed bubbles that account for the amazing light weight and other exceptional physical properties of expanded perlite. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL>The expansion process also creates one of perlite's most distinguishing characteristics: its white color. While the crude perlite rock may range from transparent to light gray to glossy black, the color of expanded perlite ranges from snowy white to grayish white. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL><b>Source</b><BR> <EOL>Our perlite comes from the Nu-Alexite and Socorro perlite deposits located in New Mexico, U.S. The ore is formed by magmatic processes, usually in ""domes,"" that over geologic time periods entrap water into the matrix. The mechanism through which this occurs is not completely understood, however, it is this entrapped water which gives perlite ore it's unique ability to expand. The Socorro deposit has proven reserves of over 10 million tons and is unique in that it is believed to be the deepest known perlite deposit in the world. These characteristics give the processed Socorro ore unmatched quality and consistency. The perlite ore is surface mined and is usually light gray in color. All perlite ore contains low crystalline silica contents in comparison with other industrial minerals. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL><b>Uses</b><BR> <EOL>Due to its low density and relatively low price, many commercial applications for perlite have developed. In the construction and manufacturing fields, it is used in lightweight plasters and mortars, insulation and ceiling tiles. <EOL><bR><BR> <EOL>Perlite is an excellent filter aid. It is used extensively as an alternative to diatomaceous earth. The popularity of perlite usage in this application is growing considerably worldwide. <EOL><bR><BR> <EOL>Although applications of perlite in art are uncommon, everyday it is being used more and more in such applications as plaster, ceramics, foundry casting and paint. <EOL><bR><BR> <EOL><b>Use in Paint</b><Br> <EOL>Perlite can be added to paint and painting mediums, especially water-based, to add bulk. For example, add perlite to calcite mediums to increase the bulk of the medium or paint for thick impastos. It can also be used to prevent settling of pigments and heavy extenders. It can also be used to add texture or build the consistency (thicken) of paint. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL><b>Use in Grounds</B><br> <EOL>A novel use of perlite is in painting grounds. Add an amount to a traditional gesso or chalk ground to increase the bulk and decrease its weight. <EOL> <EOL>",,1000000,0,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8678,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Grounds/Perlite & Technical Gelatin Bags,,7.660,,Saturday 13 November 2010 10:42:23 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,5.630,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
53775,NAP-500-22PHSP8,bhavesh,Phenol (8 fl oz) 236.6 ml Spray Bottle: Made in USA by Natural Pigments,Phenol (8 fl oz) 236.6 ml Spray Bottle by Natural Pigments,0.500,8.450,8.450,,"Phenol (8 fl oz) 236.6 ml Spray Bottle<br><br> <EOL><b>About Phenol Spray</b><Br> <EOL>Phenol Spray is a 4% solution of phenol to prevent mold formation on gesso and grounds. Add Phenol to fresh liquid gesso or chalk ground mixtures and store it for days in a cool, dark place, or for even weeks in a refrigerator, without mold forming. Add to water to prevent solutions of casein, gum arabic, skin glues, etc,. from decomposing on storage, and as a preservative or antiseptic to prevent mold growth.<br><Br> <EOL> <EOL><b>Directions of Use</b><br> <EOL><b>For Casein, Gum Arabic and Hide Glue Solutions</b><Br> <EOL> <EOL>   1. Spray Phenol generously onto the surfaces of the containers, spoons and tools that will come in contact with the solution.<br> <EOL>   2. Add Phenol to the water (1 teaspoonful to 16 fluid ounces or 500 ml) in the recipe before adding other ingredients.<br><br> <EOL> <EOL><b>For Grounds</b><Br> <EOL> <EOL>   1. Spray Phenol generously onto the surfaces of the containers, spoons and tools that will come in contact with the ground mixture.<Br> <EOL>   2. Add Phenol to the water (1 teaspoonful to 16 fluid ounces or 500 ml) in the ground recipe before adding solid ingredients.<br><br> <EOL> <EOL>Phenol (carbolic acid) has been accepted in the past as a more or less standard preservative, antiseptic, and germicide, but it has two disadvantages. It is volatile and has a powerful odor; it is customary in industry to mask this by the addition of an odorant such as oil of sassafras or some other essential oil [Ed.--clove oil is excellent for this purpose]. Despite its disadvantages, it is still employed widely, especially with gum arabic solutions, because of its thoroughly known behavior.",,999998,4,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8549,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Chemicals/Phenol,,8.620,,Monday 08 November 2010 07:33:18 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,6.340,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
58509,NAP-655-STLNDL,ariel2,Piercing Needle,Piercing Needle,0.500,2.990,2.990,,<B>Piercing Needle</b><BR><BR> <EOL><b>About Piercing Needle</b><BR> <EOL>Steel piercing needle can be used to transfer sketches to a panel by piercing the paper and then marking the design on the surface with a pounce bag. Use to inscribe designs into grounds. Also useful for scoring lines in gesso for "pastiglia" effects in gilded areas. Sturdy steel handle.,,999987,4,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8932,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Tools,,4.070,,Monday 13 December 2010 05:30:53 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,2.990,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
54023,NAP-900-PB18,bhavesh,Plastic Painting Bridge: Made in USA by Natural Pigments,Plastic Painting Bridge: Made in USA by Natural Pigments,0.500,19.500,19.500,,"* Our bridge helps prevent smudges to your painting or drawing by providing a raised rest spot for your hand or arm.<br /> <EOL>* The bridge works equally well for left- or right-handed artists.<br /> <EOL>* Our bridge meaures 455 mm (18 in.) long by 90 mm (3.5 in.) wide.<br /> <EOL>* It can easily take the full weight of your hand or arm without bending or warping.<br /> <EOL>* Specially designed for artists painting detailed work on paint panels, boards or canvases, the bridge has a 33 mm (1-5/16 in ) clearance underneath.",,999999,4,N,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8580,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Drawing Tools and Supplies/Drawing Tools & Accesories,,25.460,,Wednesday 10 November 2010 05:43:43 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,19.500,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
55044,NAP-510-32SFP1K,ariel2,Platina Dewaxed Shellac Flakes 1 kg (2.2 lb) Bag: Made in USA by Natural Pigments,Platina Dewaxed Shellac Flakes 1 kg (2.2 lb) Bag by Natural Pigments,2.200,58.880,58.880,,"Platina Dewaxed Shellac Flakes 1 kg (2.2 lb) Bag<BR><br> <EOL><b>Specifications</b><BR> <EOL>* Chemical Name: dihydroxyficocerylic acid (essential ingredient)<br> <EOL>* Chemical Formula: CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>(CHOH)(CH<sub>2</sub>)<sub>7</sub>CHOH-COOH<bR> <EOL>* Density: 1.110–1.196<br> <EOL>* Hardness: 2.0–2.5<br> <EOL>* Refractive Index: n=1.520<br><br> <EOL> <EOL><b>About Dewaxed Decolorized Shellac</b><br> <EOL>Dewaxed Decolorized Shellac is produced from carefully selected raw materials by the solvent extraction system using activated carbon. With the solvent process wax and impurities are removed by dissolving the seedlac in a solvent, usually, chilled industrial alcohol. The solution is then filtered through fine cloth and the alcohol is recovered by boiling the solution. The dissolved shellac is then stretched to the required thickness on a roller. The product obtained is called dewaxed shellac. Highly sophisticated grades of dewaxed and decolorized shellac are further processed using activated carbon as the decolorizing agent. <EOL><bR><br> <EOL><b>Uses</b><BR> <EOL>Shellac is an excellent quick-drying finish. The flake form allows fresh quality shellac to be prepared and avoids waste. Use shellac as a sealant on wood after cleaning and prior to painting or finishing, on gilding before painting or varnishing, on grounds to reduce its absorbency, and on paint to isolate layers. <EOL><bR><BR> <EOL><b>Origin and History</b><BR> <EOL>The source of shellac from lac has been known since earliest recorded times. Atharva Veda, estimated to be several thousand years old, clearly states that both lac as well as lac dye served as medicine. It was used in China as medicine as well, long before it was used in art. The original cultivation of lac for resin was for the dye that gives the resin its characteristic color. The use of the lac dye can be traced back to 250 C.E. when it was mentioned by Claudius Aelianus, a Roman writer on natural history. The use of shellac as a protective coating appears as early as 1590 in a work by an English writer who was sent to India to observe the country and its people. <EOL><bR><BR> <EOL><b>Source</b><BR> <EOL>Shellac is an animal product secreted by the Coccus lacca, a scale insect that feeds on certain trees in India and southern Asia. Soon after the scale insect is hatched, it leaves its birthplace and begins to wander about, looking for a likely place to settle down. It finds this place upon a stem or a leaf or on the outside of a fruit. It inserts its sharp little beak in the plant tissue and then settles down for a lifetime of nourishment at this spot. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL>After feeding, the insect produces a gummy substance through its pores. It is soon covered by its secretion that in certain species looks like wax, in others like cotton, in still others like powder. The growing insect sheds one skin after another; these skins form a rounded scale, held in place by the secreted substance, which hardens into a protective covering called lac. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL>This lac is collected and then it is crushed, washed and dried. After further treatment, it is skillfully drawn into thin sheets of finished shellac. The level of refinement, the timing of harvest, and source of the lac, determine the color and specifications that makes the various grades of shellac. <EOL><Br><BR> <EOL> <EOL><b>Preparation</b><BR> <EOL><b>Ingredient 	Parts by weight</b><BR> <EOL>* Shellac: 1<bR> <EOL>* Methyl alcohol: 5<Br><BR> <EOL>Denatured alcohol can be substituted for methyl alcohol (denatured alcohol is ethyl alcohol with a small amount of methyl alcohol to prevent it from being ingested), but shellac dissolves better in methyl alcohol.<br><BR> <EOL> <EOL>Combine the ingredients in a dark plastic or glass container with a tightly fitting lid. Soak the shellac flakes in about half of the total alcohol to be used for 24 hours or longer (cool room temperatures slow the process). If you are using button shellac pulverize it to hasten dissolving. Stir occasionally and when dissolved add the balance of the alcohol. <EOL><Br><BR> <EOL>After the shellac is fully dissolved, it should be strained through a fine mesh cheese cloth to remove any impurities. Shellac is made from the lac bug and a few bits of bug carcass are often left particularly in Seed Lac. Store in a cool and dark place. <EOL><br><Br> <EOL>Before the liquid shellac is used, it should be shaken or stirred thoroughly and allowed to stand for a few hours. Thinned with ten times its volume of alcohol, this solution serves well as a fixative. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL> <EOL><b>Application</b><BR> <EOL>When applying as an undercoat prior to other paint or finishes use dewaxed shellac. Dip a natural bristle brush about half way into the shellac and gently remove excess shellac against the side of the container; this gives a reasonably filled brush for full strokes without incorporating air in the shellac. Apply the shellac in long strokes. Shellac should be sanded between coats. Allow each coat to dry thoroughly. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL>If the shellac is dry, sanding will produce a fine powder on the surface. If the shellac is not dry it will be somewhat tacky to sand and the paper will clog. After sanding, wipe the surface thoroughly with a tack cloth and recoat. Depending upon temperature and humidity conditions, allow from two to four hours drying for each coat. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL>Test all mixed shellac for drying if several months old. If the surface stays tacky after 8 hours and does not sand freely without gumming, the shellac is old and will not dry and must be discarded. <EOL><Br><BR> <EOL><b>Toxicity</b><BR> <EOL>Shellac is not considered to be toxic, but care should be used in handling the ground product to avoid inhaling the dust.",,0,3,N,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8730,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Vehicles & Mediums/Shellac Flakes,,70.120,,Tuesday 16 November 2010 06:50:56 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,58.880,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
