rk plastic or glass container with a tightly fitting lid. Soak the shellac flakes in about half of the total alcohol to be used for 24 hours or longer (cool room temperatures slow the process). If you are using button shellac pulverize it to hasten dissolving. Stir occasionally and when dissolved add the balance of the alcohol. <EOL><Br><BR> <EOL>After the shellac is fully dissolved, it should be strained through a fine mesh cheese cloth to remove any impurities. Shellac is made from the lac bug and a few bits of bug carcass are often left particularly in Seed Lac. Store in a cool and dark place. <EOL><br><Br> <EOL>Before the liquid shellac is used, it should be shaken or stirred thoroughly and allowed to stand for a few hours. Thinned with ten times its volume of alcohol, this solution serves well as a fixative. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL> <EOL><b>Application</b><BR> <EOL>When applying as an undercoat prior to other paint or finishes use dewaxed shellac. Dip a natural bristle brush about half way into the shellac and gently remove excess shellac against the side of the container; this gives a reasonably filled brush for full strokes without incorporating air in the shellac. Apply the shellac in long strokes. Shellac should be sanded between coats. Allow each coat to dry thoroughly. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL>If the shellac is dry, sanding will produce a fine powder on the surface. If the shellac is not dry it will be somewhat tacky to sand and the paper will clog. After sanding, wipe the surface thoroughly with a tack cloth and recoat. Depending upon temperature and humidity conditions, allow from two to four hours drying for each coat. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL>Test all mixed shellac for drying if several months old. If the surface stays tacky after 8 hours and does not sand freely without gumming, the shellac is old and will not dry and must be discarded. <EOL><Br><BR> <EOL><b>Toxicity</b><BR> <EOL>Shellac is not considered to be toxic, but care should be used in handling the ground product to avoid inhaling the dust.",,0,2,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8730,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Vehicles & Mediums/Shellac Flakes,,55.040,,Tuesday 16 November 2010 07:11:43 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,42.500,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
58529,NAP-681-11102,ariel2,Pointing Trowel: 5 1/2" X 1 7/8",Pointing Trowel: 5 1/2" X 1 7/8",0.500,13.500,13.500,,"<B>Pointing Trowel: 5 1/2"" X 1 7/8"" </b><BR><BR> <EOL><b>About Pointing Trowel</b><BR> <EOL>The bullnose pointing trowel is for polishing the final layer of plaster just prior to transferring the cartoon and for polishing the painted fresco. It is also useful for applying plaster in tight or curved areas. Pointing trowels are handy tools for patchwork and for cleaning other tools. <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL>The blade, post and tang is forged from a single piece of high-grade tool steel for maximum strength. The hardwood handle is held in place by a polished steel ferrule. The handle rise is set for the correct ""hang"" and balance. The blade is taper-ground and polished to give it the right amount of flexibility. <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL>The lengths of pointing trowels range from 11,5 cm to 17,75 cm (4-1/2 to 7 inches), the most popular sizes being 12.7 and 15.2 cm (5- and 6-inch) lengths.",,999999,1,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8932,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Tools,,18.060,,Monday 13 December 2010 06:05:43 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,13.500,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
58366,NAP-624-420705,bhavesh,"Porcelain 10-Well Rectangular Porcelain Palette, 6.5 in. x 3.5 in. (170 x 85 mm)","Porcelain 10-Well Rectangular Porcelain Palette, 6.5 in. x 3.5 in",0.100,8.990,8.990,,"<B>Porcelain 10-Well Palette</b><BR> <EOL><B>About Rectangular Palette</b><BR> <EOL>This Rectangular Palette features five round wells for holding colors and five rectangular slant wells for mixing colors. A watermedia artist's favorite, this ceramic palette is made of hand-glazed porcelain. It is easy to rinse clean and never stains.",,999995,5,N,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8932,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Tools,,12.120,,Saturday 11 December 2010 07:53:36 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,8.990,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
58381,NAP-624-420707,bhavesh2,Porcelain 7-Well Flower Palette 12 cm (4.5 in.) Diameter,Porcelain 7-Well Flower Palette 12 cm (4.5 in.) Diameter,0.100,6,6,,"<B>Porcelain 7-Well Flower Palette</b><BR> <EOL><B>About Porcelain 7-Well Flower Palette</b><BR> <EOL>This seven-well, round porcelain palette is floral-shape dish measuring 4.5 inches in diameter and 1/2 inch in depth. A watermedia artist's favorite, these ceramic dishes are made of glazed porcelain. They are easy to rinse clean and never stain.",,1000000,1,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8932,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Tools,,8.160,,Saturday 11 December 2010 08:14:31 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,6,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
58436,NAP-624-WCW213,bhavesh2,Porcelain 7-Well Palette with Cover 18 cm (7 in.) Diameter,Porcelain 7-Well Palette with Cover 18 cm (7 in.) Diameter,0.100,11.500,11.500,,"<B>Porcelain 7-Well Palette with Cover </b><BR><BR> <EOL><B>About Porcelain 7-Well Palette </b><BR> <EOL>This seven-well, round porcelain palette is floral-shape dish measuring 7-inches in diameter and 5/8-inch in depth. A watermedia artist's favorite, these ceramic dishes are made of glazed porcelain. They are easy to rinse clean and never stain.",,1000000,0,N,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8459,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments,,13.610,,Saturday 11 December 2010 10:19:44 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,5.980,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
58385,NAP-624-420708,bhavesh2,Porcelain 9-Well Palette 20 cm (8 in.) Diameter,Porcelain 9-Well Palette 20 cm (8 in.) Diameter,0.100,9.500,9.500,,"<B>Porcelain 9-Well Palette 20 cm (8 in.) Diameter</b><BR><BR> <EOL><B>About Porcelain 9-Well Palette</b><BR> <EOL>This nine-well, round porcelain palette is floral-shape dish measuring 8-inches in diameter and 1/2-inch in depth. A watermedia artist's favorite, these ceramic dishes are made of glazed porcelain. They are easy to rinse clean and never stain.",,1000000,0,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8932,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Tools,,12.810,,Saturday 11 December 2010 08:21:48 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,9.500,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
58392,NAP-624-420711,bhavesh2,Porcelain Brush Rest,Porcelain Brush Rest,0.500,5.500,5.500,,<B>Porcelain Brush Rest</b><BR><BR> <EOL><B>About Porcelain Brush Rest</b><BR> <EOL>This simple but elegant brush rest has several notches to receive the uppermost part of a paint brush to prevent paint smears and to protect and extend the life of the brush. The brush rest is weighted so that it will not slide off your table. Hand glazed pure white porcelain will not stain with paints or dyes. Dimensions: 5.5 W x 1 D x 2 H inches (14 x 2.5 x 5 cm). This is a practical item for tempera and watercolor painters. It is also makes a nice companion gift for brushes.,,999998,1,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8932,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Tools,,7.480,,Saturday 11 December 2010 08:32:39 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,5.500,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
58404,NAP-624-GIANT,bhavesh,"Porcelain Giant 21-Well Rectangular Porcelain Palette, 16 x 12"" (41 x 31 cm)","Porcelain Giant 21-Well Rectangular Porcelain Palette, 16 x 12 inches",8,45,45,,"<B>Porcelain Giant 21-Well Rectangular Porcelain Palette</b><BR><BR> <EOL><B>Large Folding Palette</b><BR> <EOL>This large palette features 7 rectangular wells each on three sides and a large mixing trough and brush rest on the fourth side. In the center is a large area for mixing colors. A watermedia artist's favorite, this ceramic palette is made of hand-glazed porcelain. It is easy to rinse clean and never stains.",,1000000,0,N,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8932,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Tools,,58.270,,Saturday 11 December 2010 08:52:50 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,45,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
58432,NAP-624-WCW210,bhavesh,Porcelain Small Nesting Bowls 7 cm (2 3/4 in.) Diameter,Porcelain Small Nesting Bowls 7 cm (2 3/4 in.) Diameter,0.100,16.780,16.780,,"<B>Porcelain Small Nesting Bowls</b><BR><BR> <EOL><B>About Porcelain Small Nesting Bowls</b><BR> <EOL>Small nesting porcelain bowls, a watermedia artist's favorite, these ceramic dishes are made of hand-glazed porcelain. They are easy to rinse clean and will not stain.",,999999,1,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8932,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Tools,,22.090,,Saturday 11 December 2010 10:16:24 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,16.780,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
53758,NAP-500-21POH1K,bhavesh,Potassium Hydroxide (Caustic Potash) 1 Kg (2.2 lb) Bag: Made in USA by Natural Pigments,Potassium Hydroxide (Caustic Potash) 1 Kg (2.2 lb) by Natural Pigments,1.100,20,20,,"Potassium Hydroxide (Caustic Potash) 1 Kg (2.2 lb) Bag<br><br> <EOL><b>Specifications</b><Br> <EOL>* Chemical Name: Potassium Hydroxide<br> <EOL>* Chemical Formula: KOH<br> <EOL>* CAS No.: 1310-58-3<br> <EOL>* Mesh (%): Flakes<br> <EOL>* Color: White solid <br><BR> <EOL> <EOL><b>About Potassium Hydroxide</b><br>  <EOL>Potassium Hydroxide, also known as caustic potash, is a strong alkali. It is usually sold as translucent flakes or pellets, which become tacky when exposed to air because it is hygroscopic. Consequently, it characteristically contains varying amounts of water, as well as carbonates. Its dissolution in water is strongly exothermic, meaning the process gives off significant heat. The concentrated aqueous solution of potassium hydroxide is called potassium lye or potash lye.<br><BR> <EOL> <EOL>Potassium hydroxide is used in making lake pigments, to saponify (form soap) oil, wax or resin, such as rosinate for varnishes, and to hydrolyze casein to make tempera paint. It can be substituted with sodium hydroxide, another strong alkali.<br><BR> <EOL> <EOL><b>Solubility</b><br> <EOL>Approximately 121 g of potassium hydroxide will dissolve in 100 mL of water at room temperature (compared with 100 g of sodium hydroxide in the same volume). Lower alcohols such as methanol, ethanol, and propanols are also excellent solvents. The solubility in ethanol is about 40 g potassium hydroxide in 100 mL ethyl alcohol.<br><BR> <EOL> <EOL><b>Saponification</b><br> <EOL>The saponification of fats with potassium hydroxide is used to prepare ""potassium soaps,"" which are softer than the more common sodium hydroxide-derived soaps. Because of their softness and greater solubility, potassium soaps require less water to liquefy, and can thus contain more cleaning agent than liquefied sodium soaps.<br><BR> <EOL> <EOL><b>Storage</b><Br> <EOL>Keep in a tightly closed container, stored in a cool, dry, ventilated area. Protect against physical damage. Isolate from incompatible substances. Protect from moisture. Addition to water releases heat which can result in violent boiling and spattering. Always add slowly and in small amounts. Never use hot water. Containers of this material may be hazardous when empty since they retain product residues (dust, solids); observe all warnings and precautions listed for the product. <EOL><br><br> <EOL><b>Safe Handling</b><BR> <EOL>Wear impervious protective clothing, including boots, gloves, apron or coveralls, as appropriate, to prevent skin contact. Use chemical safety goggles and/or full face shield where dusting or splashing of solutions is possible. Refer to Material Safety Data Sheet. <EOL><br><Br> <EOL> <EOL><b> <Font color=red>DANGER! CORROSIVE. CAUSES SEVERE BURNS TO SKIN, EYES, RESPIRATORY TRACT, AND GASTROINTESTINAL TRACT. MATERIAL IS EXTREMELY DESTRUCTIVE TO ALL BODY TISSUES. MAY BE FATAL IF SWALLOWED. HARMFUL IF INHALED.</Font></b><br><BR> <EOL> <EOL><b>Environmental Hazards</b><Br> <EOL>This product is toxic to aquatic organisms, and may cause long-term adverse effects in the aquatic environment. If product is spilled, comply with Federal, State and local regulations on reporting spills. Refer to Material Safety Sheet and labels for further details. <EOL>",,999995,7,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8540,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Chemicals/Potassium Hydroxide,,18.090,,Monday 08 November 2010 05:29:34 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,15,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
53759,NAP-500-21POH50,bhavesh,Potassium Hydroxide (Caustic Potash) 500 g (1.1 lb) Bag: Made in USA by Natural Pigments,Potassium Hydroxide (Caustic Potash) 1.1 lb Bag by Natural Pigments,1.100,12,12,,"Potassium Hydroxide (Caustic Potash) 500 g (1.1 lb) Bag<br><br> <EOL><b>Specifications</b><Br> <EOL>* Chemical Name: Potassium Hydroxide<br> <EOL>* Chemical Formula: KOH<br> <EOL>* CAS No.: 1310-58-3<br> <EOL>* Mesh (%): Flakes<br> <EOL>* Color: White solid <br><BR> <EOL> <EOL><b>About Potassium Hydroxide</b><br>  <EOL>Potassium Hydroxide, also known as caustic potash, is a strong alkali. It is usually sold as translucent flakes or pellets, which become tacky when exposed to air because it is hygroscopic. Consequently, it characteristically contains varying amounts of water, as well as carbonates. Its dissolution in water is strongly exothermic, meaning the process gives off significant heat. The concentrated aqueous solution of potassium hydroxide is called potassium lye or potash lye.<br><BR> <EOL> <EOL>Potassium hydroxide is used in making lake pigments, to saponify (form soap) oil, wax or resin, such as rosinate for varnishes, and to hydrolyze casein to make tempera paint. It can be substituted with sodium hydroxide, another strong alkali.<br><BR> <EOL> <EOL><b>Solubility</b><br> <EOL>Approximately 121 g of potassium hydroxide will dissolve in 100 mL of water at room temperature (compared with 100 g of sodium hydroxide in the same volume). Lower alcohols such as methanol, ethanol, and propanols are also excellent solvents. The solubility in ethanol is about 40 g potassium hydroxide in 100 mL ethyl alcohol.<br><BR> <EOL> <EOL><b>Saponification</b><br> <EOL>The saponification of fats with potassium hydroxide is used to prepare ""potassium soaps,"" which are softer than the more common sodium hydroxide-derived soaps. Because of their softness and greater solubility, potassium soaps require less water to liquefy, and can thus contain more cleaning agent than liquefied sodium soaps.<br><BR> <EOL> <EOL><b>Storage</b><Br> <EOL>Keep in a tightly closed container, stored in a cool, dry, ventilated area. Protect against physical damage. Isolate from incompatible substances. Protect from moisture. Addition to water releases heat which can result in violent boiling and spattering. Always add slowly and in small amounts. Never use hot water. Containers of this material may be hazardous when empty since they retain product residues (dust, solids); observe all warnings and precautions listed for the product. <EOL><br><br> <EOL><b>Safe Handling</b><BR> <EOL>Wear impervious protective clothing, including boots, gloves, apron or coveralls, as appropriate, to prevent skin contact. Use chemical safety goggles and/or full face shield where dusting or splashing of solutions is possible. Refer to Material Safety Data Sheet. <EOL><br><Br> <EOL> <EOL><b> <Font color=red>DANGER! CORROSIVE. CAUSES SEVERE BURNS TO SKIN, EYES, RESPIRATORY TRACT, AND GASTROINTESTINAL TRACT. MATERIAL IS EXTREMELY DESTRUCTIVE TO ALL BODY TISSUES. MAY BE FATAL IF SWALLOWED. HARMFUL IF INHALED.</Font></b><br><BR> <EOL> <EOL><b>Environmental Hazards</b><Br> <EOL>This product is toxic to aquatic organisms, and may cause long-term adverse effects in the aquatic environment. If product is spilled, comply with Federal, State and local regulations on reporting spills. Refer to Material Safety Sheet and labels for further details. <EOL>",,999991,12,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8540,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Chemicals/Potassium Hydroxide,,12.140,,Monday 08 November 2010 05:41:41 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,9,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
54823,NAP-510-13POZ1K,bhavesh2,Pozzolana 1 Kg (2.2 lbs) Bag: Made in USA by Natural Pigments,Pozzolana 1 Kg (2.2 lbs) Bag by Natural Pigments,2.200,6.850,6.850,,"Pozzolana 1 Kg (2.2 lbs) Bag<BR><br> <EOL><b>About Pozzolana </b><br> <EOL>Pozzolana is ground volcanic ash composed of siliceous and aluminous material from Pozzuoli, Italy. <EOL><Br><BR> <EOL>Pozzolana (or pozzolan) is an Italian word, named from Pozzuoli, the place near Naples where pozzolan was first mined and used as cement, during Roman times. Pozzolan is a fine-grained pumicious material, which combines with lime to make a smooth, plaster-like cement. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL>Use pozzolana with lime putty in fresco painting to make hydraulic plaster for stronger fresco walls. Pozzolana reacts with slaked lime and water to form mortar with cementious properties. The fine grind (1-100 microns) of this pozzolana is best used in the brown and intonaco coasts of fresco. It can also be added to give ""tooth"" and color to grounds and paint.",,999992,6,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8679,"Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Grounds/Pozzolana, Rottenstone & Bentonite with Bags",,9.310,,Monday 15 November 2010 06:48:51 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,6.850,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
54824,NAP-510-13POZ5K,rommel,Pozzolana 5 Kg (11 lbs) Bag: Made in USA by Natural Pigments,Pozzolana 5 Kg (11 lbs) Bag: Made in USA by Natural Pigments,11,27.500,27.500,,"Pozzolana 5 Kg (11 lbs) Bag<BR><br> <EOL><b>About Pozzolana </b><br> <EOL>Pozzolana is ground volcanic ash composed of siliceous and aluminous material from Pozzuoli, Italy. <EOL><br><br> <EOL>Pozzolana (or pozzolan) is an Italian word, named from Pozzuoli, the place near Naples where pozzolan was first mined and used as cement, during Roman times. Pozzolan is a fine-grained pumicious material, which combines with lime to make a smooth, plaster-like cement. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL>Use pozzolana with lime putty in fresco painting to make hydraulic plaster for stronger fresco walls. Pozzolana reacts with slaked lime and water to form mortar with cementious properties. The fine grind (1-100 microns) of this pozzolana is best used in the brown and intonaco coasts of fresco. It can also be added to give ""tooth"" and color to grounds and paint.",,999991,3,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,57728,Painting and Painting Accessories/Raw Materials/Pigments,,35.620,,Monday 15 November 2010 06:52:31 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,27.500,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
54820,NAP-510-13PUM1K,bhavesh,Pumice Grade 0 (Medium 1 Kg) Bag: Made in USA by Natural Pigments,Pumice Grade 0 (Medium 1 Kg) Bag: Made in USA by Natural Pigments,2.200,11,11,,"Pumice Grade 0 (Medium 1 Kg) Bag<br><br> <EOL><b>Specifications</b><Br> <EOL><b>Typical Physical Properties</b><Br> <EOL>* Reflectance (green filter): 80<br> <EOL>* Refractive Index: 1.5<br> <EOL>* pH (10% in water): 9<br> <EOL>* Specific Gravity: 2.4<br> <EOL>* Weight per Solid Gallon, lbs: 20.0<br> <EOL>* Bulking Volume, gal/lb: 0.5<br> <EOL>* Apparent Bulk Density, lbs/cu ft: 47.0<br> <EOL>* Hardness, Mohs: 5.5<br> <EOL>* Moisture, %: <1<br> <EOL>* Oil Absorption: 45 g oil / 100 g<br><br> <EOL> <EOL><b>Typical Chemical Analysis</b><BR> <EOL>* Silicon Dioxide (SiO<sub>2</sub>): 75<br> <EOL>* Aluminum Oxide (Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>): 12<bR> <EOL>* Phosphate (K<sub>2</sub>O): 4<br> <EOL>* Sodium Oxide (Na<sub>2</sub>O): 4<br> <EOL>* Iron Oxide (Fe<sub>2</sub>O): <2<br> <EOL>* Calcium Oxide (CaO): <2<br> <EOL>* Magnesium Oxide (MgO): <2<br> <EOL>* Titanium Dioxide (TiO<sub>2</sub>): <2<br><br> <EOL> <EOL><b>Sieve Analysis</b><BR> <EOL><table cellspacing=""2"" cellpadding=""2"" border=""1""> <EOL> <EOL><tr> <EOL><td>Size No.</td> <EOL><td>Opening Inches</td> <EOL><td>Opening Microns</td> <EOL><td>% Retained</td> <EOL></tr> <EOL> <EOL><tr> <EOL><td>80</td> <EOL><td>0.0069</td> <EOL><td>177</td> <EOL><td><1</td> <EOL></tr> <EOL><tr> <EOL><td>100</td> <EOL><td>0.0058 </td> <EOL><td>149</td> <EOL><td><1</td> <EOL></tr> <EOL><tr> <EOL><td>120</td> <EOL><td>0.0049</td> <EOL><td>125</td> <EOL><td>1-10</td> <EOL></tr> <EOL><tr> <EOL><td>140</td> <EOL><td>0.0041</td> <EOL><td>105</td> <EOL><td>1-15</td> <EOL></tr> <EOL><tr> <EOL><td>170 </td> <EOL><td>0.0035</td> <EOL><td>88</td> <EOL><td>1-15</td> <EOL></tr> <EOL> <EOL><tr> <EOL><td>200</td> <EOL><td>0.0029 </td> <EOL><td>74</td> <EOL><td>1-15<1</td> <EOL></tr> <EOL> <EOL><tr> <EOL><td>325</td> <EOL><td>0.0017</td> <EOL><td>44</td> <EOL><td>10-25</td> <EOL></tr> <EOL> <EOL><tr> <EOL><td>Pan</td> <EOL><td>>0.0017</td> <EOL><td>>4</td> <EOL><td>40-70</td> <EOL></tr> <EOL> <EOL><tr> <EOL><td>TOTAL </td> <EOL><td></td> <EOL><td></td> <EOL><td>100%</td> <EOL></tr> <EOL></table> <EOL><br> <EOL> <EOL><b>About Pumice</b><BR> <EOL>Our pumice is a near-white, milled volcanic glass that has many uses in fine arts. Add pumice to paint to make it appear mat when dried, to add texture and to increase body without tinting the color. Pumice can also be added to grounds to give it ""tooth"" and as an abrasive to smooth and polish substrates and grounds. <EOL><bR><BR> <EOL>This grade of white pumice is used to polish surfaces such as wood, and added to chalk and gesso grounds to give it tooth. This grade of pumice is white so it will not discolor grounds. Use this grade to add fine texture to paint and to give it a matt appearance. <EOL><Br><br> <EOL>Pumice is formed when lava from an erupting volcano flows onto the earth's surface and cools. The comparatively low atmospheric pressure at the surface results in the sudden release of steam and other dissolved gases from the still molten mass, thereby producing bubbles. This creates the pocked appearance familiar in ""lava rock"" used for gas grill briquettes and landscape rock. Pumice deposits are only found within a few miles of ancient volcanoes. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL>Our pumice is from a deposit located in Espanola, New Mexico. The ore is mined from open pits and then transported to the plant where it is crushed, milled and dried. It is then processed through a modern classification system to produce a rigorously controlled and consistently uniform product. <EOL>",,1000000,0,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8674,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Grounds/Pumices Bags & Bottles,,14.830,,Monday 15 November 2010 06:43:38 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,11,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
