 bovine collagen are comparable. Collagen glues designated as ""rabbit skin glue"" have the highest gel strength, while those designated as ""hide glue"" is intermediate between that and glues named ""technical gelatin,"" which has the lowest gel strength. Naming it ""rabbit skin"" glue, however, does not mean it actually consists of rabbit hides. We follow this practice, because if we named the product according to its source, then it would be called ""primarily bovine hide glue,"" in which case artists would not be able to identify what they need. However, unlike most sellers of these products, we have identified the source of the collagen glue. <EOL><br><br> <EOL>Always make the minimum concentration required; as a guide, a set jelly should be somewhere between hard set and liquid. For a canvas or panel size, try 40 grams of rabbit skin glue for every quart of water. For distemper paints, 60 grams for every quart. As an adhesive, check the consistency by dipping a piece of wood into the glue pot. If the glue runs off smoothly, you've got it right. If it is too thick, add a little water. Use the glue hot.<br><BR> <EOL> <EOL> <EOL><b>Glue Size Recipe</b><BR> <EOL> <EOL><b>Ingredients</B><BR> <EOL>* 50 grams rabbit skin glue (dry)<br> <EOL>* 1 liter water<br> <EOL>* 5 grams alum<br><br> <EOL> <EOL>* Prepare rabbit skin glue by soaking 50 grams of rabbit skin glue in 800 ml of water for approximately 2 hours. You can also leave it overnight.<br> <EOL>* Add 5 grams of alum to 200 ml of water and let it dissolve. Add the alum solution to the warm glue before applying it on the panel or canvas. The alum will make the sizing water resistant and form a jelly-like consistency once the glue has had time to cool down.<br> <EOL>* Apply the glue as a jelly, in a single, thin layer by using a spatula.<br> <EOL>* Let the size dry for approximately 24 hours.<br><bR> <EOL> <EOL><b>Distemper Paint Recipe</b><BR> <EOL> <EOL>Hide glue and gelatine both provide a low cost, easily formulated paint which is called distemper. Diluted with water, it is good for color sketching, as well as for painting. Distemper paintings have lasted for centuries without change. <EOL><Br><BR> <EOL><b>Ingredients</b><BR> <EOL>* 1 part hide glue (dry)<br> <EOL>* 10 parts water<br><br> <EOL>* Leave the glue in water overnight or for a full day.<br> <EOL>* Let the glue absorb as much water as it can, then pour off the excess water.<br> <EOL>* Warm this swollen glue in a double boiler or glue pot. This will cause it to melt. All hide glues should never be heated over 150° F.<br><BR> <EOL> <EOL><b>How to Use</b><br> <EOL>Work the dry pigments with water into a heavy paste with a pallete knife. Then grind the pigment into the warm solution of glue. Keep the paints warm enough to remain in solution while painting with them, and use warm water to dilute them. Use a bristle brush for painting, applying the paint in thin layers to glue-sized paper, cardboard, panel, or canvas. This method is excellent for alla prima painting and for thin underpainting. To harden and preserve the paint film, spray the dried painting with a 10% solution of water and alum.<bR><br> <EOL> <EOL><b>Note 1:</b> The millipascal seconds is a measurement of viscosity which is measured by the flow velocity of the glue solution through a funnel. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL><b>Note 2:</b> The bloom measurement refers to the elasticity of a gelatinous mass. The higher the number the greater the elasticity. <EOL> <EOL>",,999991,5,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,50393,Adhesives and Fasteners/Glues,,24.320,,Monday 15 November 2010 07:09:30 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,20.250,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
58420,NAP-624-PNTRRCT,bhavesh2,Rectangular Palette,Rectangular Palette,0.100,0.590,0.590,,"<B>Rectangular Palette</b><BR><BR> <EOL><B>About Rectangular Palette</b><BR> <EOL>White rectangular plastic palette, 3.5 in. x 5.25 in. (9 x 13.5 cm), for holding paint in 6 isolated round wells in 2 columns by 3 rows.",,1000000,0,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8932,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Tools,,0.810,,Saturday 11 December 2010 09:30:41 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,0.590,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
58395,NAP-624-420728,bhavesh,Rectangular Porcelain 28-Well Palette: 7.25 in. x 6 in. (184 x 152 mm),Rectangular Porcelain 28-Well Palette: 7.25 in. x 6 in. (184 x 152 mm),0.500,15,15,,"<B>Rectangular Porcelain 28-Well Palette</b><BR><BR> <EOL><B>About Rectangular Porcelain 28-Well Palette</b><BR> <EOL>This porcelain palette is idea for the watercolorist or tempera painter preparing many precise tints of colors for painting. Originally designed for photo retouching, it features 28 small round wells. A watermedia artist's favorite, this ceramic palette is made of hand-glazed porcelain. It is easy to rinse clean and never stains.",,1000000,0,N,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8932,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Tools,,19.910,,Saturday 11 December 2010 08:37:37 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,15,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
55176,NAP-510-41ORL08,bhavesh,Refined Aged Linseed Oil 8 oz (237 ml) Bottle: Made in USA by Natural Pigments,Refined Aged Linseed Oil 8 oz (237 ml) Bottle by Natural Pigments,0.500,9.500,9.500,,"Refined Aged Linseed Oil 8 oz (237 ml) Bottle<BR><br> <EOL><b>Specifications</b><BR> <EOL>* Natural Source: Linseed Linum usitatissimum<bR> <EOL>* Acid Value (mg KOH/g), max: 0.20 <br> <EOL>* Color (Gardner), max.: 6 <br> <EOL>* Iodine Value: 183.9 <br> <EOL>* Viscosity (Gardner-Holdt @ 25° C): -<br> <EOL>* Appearance: Clear/Transparent at 25° C<br> <EOL>* Specific gravity: 0.923 @ 25° C<br> <EOL>* Weight Per Gallon: 7.71 lbs.<br><BR> <EOL> <EOL><b>About Aged Linseed Oil</b><BR> <EOL>Natural Pigments refined aged linseed oil is a low-acid value drying oil pressed from North American flaxseed. This is as light and pure as industrially-produced linseed oil can be made. Use to grind colors, thin oil colors or prepare varnishes. <EOL><bR><BR> <EOL>Linseed oil is a yellowish drying oil derived from the dried ripe seeds of the flax plant (Linum usitatissimum, Linaceae)<br><bR> <EOL> <EOL><b>Warning:</b><BR> Rags or paper towels contaminated with vegetable drying oils, particularly those containing iron oxide pigments are susceptible to spontaneous combustion. To prevent unexpected fires, used rags or paper towels contaminated with oil-based materials should be collected in a closeable, air-tight container. Store water-dampened rags or paper towels in a metal container with an air-tight top. Alternately, washing contaminated rags will remove contaminating materials and eliminate risk. <EOL>",,999996,1,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8727,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Vehicles & Mediums/Oils,,9.690,,Tuesday 16 November 2010 10:20:59 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,7.130,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
59360,NAP-671-100S,bhavesh2,Reine Kernseife (Pure Curd Soap) 100 g Bar,Reine Kernseife (Pure Curd Soap) 100 g Bar,0.100,3.950,3.950,,"Reine Kernseife (Pure Curd Soap) 100 g Bar<BR><br> <EOL><b>About Curd soap</b><Br> <EOL>Curd soap is made without fillers, colorants, fragrances, optical brighteners and without chelating agents, such as EDTA, and synthetic surfactants. This simple, hardworking and long lasting soap is made from pure palm and coconut oil -- 70% of the soap composition. Coconut oil contributes cleaning power even in cold and hard water, while palm oil gives the soap hardness, which prevents it from softening quickly when wet, making a longer lasting soap. Before being put on sale, it is thoroughly matured to increase its durability. <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL>This is an ideal soap for cleaning artists brushes and has been favored among European painters for hundreds of years. The soap gives a slight alkaline reaction in water and will not create much lather, but cleanses well.<BR><BR> <EOL>Curd soaps are hardworking cleansers. Curd soaps are not 'superfatted' and, in contrast to cold-stirred soaps, do not contain glycerin. It does not contain lanolin, oils, emollients, dyes and perfumes, which are added to other soaps to make them gentler and more pleasing for washing the body. Our curd soap is made pure without additives, so it performs better when cleaning brushes. <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL>Other soaps are made by boiling fatty oils and fats with alkaline solutions so that the result is a mixture of soap with water, excess alkali and glycerin liberated from the oil. In curd soap, on the other hand, the uncombined alkali and glycerin are separated by ""salting out,"" and the soap contains less than 20-25% water. <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL>The difference is in the preparation; our curd soap is made according to the method used for several hundred years by European soap makers. Curd soap is prepared by heating purified vegetable oil or animal fat with sodium hydroxide and water. Upon boiling, common salt (sodium chloride) is added to the liquid, so the soap separates as a curd. This was the origin of the term ""curd soap"" (German: Kernseife).<BR><BR> <EOL><B>Pure Curd Soap</b><BR> <EOL>5 x 7.5 x 2.7 cm (2 x 3 x 1.25 inch) off-white soap cake<BR><BR> <EOL> <EOL><B>Embossed on the front side:</b><BR> <EOL>Riene Kernseife / Pure Curd Soap<BR><BR> <EOL> <EOL><B>Embossed on the back side:</B><BR> <EOL>Frischgew 100g / Fresh weight 100g<BR> <EOL>Fettgehalt 70% / Fat 70%<BR> <EOL>ENZIAN Metzingen / ENZIAN (Enzian-Seifen GmbH) Metzingen (Germany)<BR><BR> <EOL> <EOL><B>Cleaning Brushes</b><BR> <EOL>The proper use, cleaning and care of brushes is essential to ensure they last a long time. The best way to keep brushes in good working condition is to wash the paint completely out of the hair immediately after painting. If paint is allowed to dry in the brush, the hair will harden and render the brush unusable. Here are some tips to help you properly clean your brushes: <EOL><Br><BR> <EOL>Water-soluble Paints: Rinse with water, then wash with warm water and curd soap. <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL><B>Acrylic Paints:</b><BR> Keep the hair of the brushes in water to keep colors wet. If acrylic paint is allowed to dry in the tuft, the brush will be ruined. Rinse with water, then wash with warm water and curd soap while still wet. If the acrylic paint dries in the hair, use a special detergent for that purpose. <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL><B>Oil Paints:</b><BR> First, wipe off the excess paint from the brush with a rag or paper towel. Wash remaining color out of the brush hair or bristle with warm water and curd soap. <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL>After cleaning, always bring the hair back to the original brush shape. Reshape the tuft with the fingertips and allow to dry standing in an upright position. <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL>Before using the brush again, let it dry completely.",,999989,8,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8932,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Tools,,5.370,,Saturday 18 December 2010 05:16:44 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,3.950,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
54352,NAP-320-GSWB,bhavesh2,Rolco Aquasize Water-Based Gilding (16 oz): Made in USA by Natural Pigments,Rolco Aquasize Water-Based Gilding (16 oz) by Natural Pigments,0.010,14.450,14.450,,"Rolco Aquasize Water-Based Gilding (16 oz)<br><br> <EOL><b> About Rolco Aquasize</b><BR> <EOL>Aquasize is a water-based, gilding adhesive (size) designed for all interior and vertical exterior leaf work where extended tack time is required. Emission safe. No fire hazard. Minimum toxicity.",,1000000,4,N,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8459,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments,,15.610,,Friday 12 November 2010 05:59:15 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,10.840,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
54353,NAP-320-GSWS,bhavesh2,Rolco Aquasize Water-Based Gilding (8 oz): Made in USA by Natural Pigments,Rolco Aquasize Water-Based Gilding (8 oz) by Natural Pigments,0.010,7.950,7.950,,"Rolco Aquasize Water-Based Gilding (8 oz)<br><br> <EOL><b> About Rolco Aquasize</b><BR> <EOL>Aquasize is a water-based, gilding adhesive (size) designed for all interior and vertical exterior leaf work where extended tack time is required. Emission safe. No fire hazard. Minimum toxicity.",,1000000,3,N,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8459,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments,,8.490,,Friday 12 November 2010 06:04:11 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,5.960,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
58453,NAP-630-RWE12,bhavesh,Rosewood Easel (12-inch),Rosewood Easel (12-inch),0.100,12,12,,<B>Rosewood Easel (12-inch)</b><BR><BR> <EOL><B>About Rosewood Easel </b><BR> <EOL>Triple routed and lacquered with real brass hinges. Ideal for use while painting and for display. This small wooden stand will support your painting panel (up to 9.5 by 12.5 in.) in an upright position (appox. 30 degrees from vertical) while painting and after it is completed for display.,,999998,2,N,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,57297,Easels/Display and Presentation Easels,,16.050,,Saturday 11 December 2010 10:39:27 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,12,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
58451,NAP-630-RWE06,bhavesh,Rosewood Easel (6-inch) Made in USA by Natural Pigments,Rosewood Easel (6-inch) Made in USA by Natural Pigments,0.100,5.950,5.950,,<B>Rosewood Easel (6-inch) Made in USA by Natural Pigments</b><BR><BR> <EOL><B>About Rosewood Easel </b><BR> <EOL>Triple routed and lacquered with real brass hinges. Ideal for use while painting and for display. This small wooden stand will support your painting panel (up to 9.5 by 12.5 in.) in an upright position (appox. 30 degrees from vertical) while painting and after it is completed for display.,,1000000,0,N,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,54363,Easels/Wooden Easels,,8.090,,Saturday 11 December 2010 10:35:44 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,5.950,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
54788,NAP-510-13ROT50,bhavesh,Rottenstone 500 g (17.64 oz) Bag: Made in USA by Natural Pigments,Rottenstone 500 g (17.64 oz) Bag: Made in USA by Natural Pigments,1.100,14.500,14.500,,"Rottenstone (500 g) 17.64 oz Bag<BR><br> <EOL><b>About Rottenstone</b><bR> <EOL>Rottenstone (sometimes spelled as two words and also known as ""tripoli"") is weathered limestone that is mixed with diatomaceous, amorphous or crystalline silica. It is often used for polishing metals and distressing water gilded surfaces to reveal bole and lay-lines. The grey color makes it a desirable antiquing dust. It has similar applications to pumice, but it is a finer powder and used for a more glossy polish after an initial treatment with (fine granularity) pumice powder. It is usually mixed with oil, sometimes water, and rubbed on the surface of varnished or lacquered wood with a felt pad or cloth. Rotten stone is sometimes used to buff stains out of wood. Some polishing waxes contain powdered rotten stone in a paste substrate. For larger polishing jobs, rotten stone mixed with a binder is applied to polishing wheels. <EOL>",,1000000,3,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,57366,Painting and Painting Accessories/Acrylic Painting/Artist Acrylics/Heavy Body Acrylics,,14.670,,Monday 15 November 2010 05:48:33 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,10.880,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
58430,NAP-624-PNTRRND,bhavesh2,Round Palette,Round Palette,0.100,1.290,1.290,,"<B>Round Palette</b><BR><BR> <EOL><B>About Rectangular Palette</b><BR> <EOL>White round plastic palette, 7.5 in. (19 cm) diameter, for paint in 10 isolated round wells around the edge and one large well in the center.",,1000000,0,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8932,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Tools,,1.760,,Saturday 11 December 2010 10:12:55 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,1.290,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
58548,NAP-681-14408,bhavesh,Rubber Float: 12" X 3 1/2",Rubber Float: 12" X 3 1/2",0.500,7.500,7.500,,"<B>Rubber Float: 12"" X 3 1/2""</b><BR><BR> <EOL><b>About Rubber Float</b><BR> <EOL>Floats are used to assist in preparing the surface for troweling, or if a float (rough) finish is desired, floating is the final operation. <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL>This rubber float has a molded black rubber pad that is very comfortable and has a dense texture. The float face is permanently cemented to the lightweight aluminum backing. The plastic handle is both comfortable and durable. This is an ideal float for bringing sand to the surface. <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL>Floats are made of aluminum, magnesium, wood, cork and rubber. The best selling sizes are 30.5 X 12.7 cm (12 X 5 inches).",,1000000,0,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8932,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Tools,,10.190,,Monday 13 December 2010 06:45:06 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,7.500,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
55277,NAP-520-1OMS16,bhavesh2,Rublesol Odorless Mineral Spirits (16 oz) 473 ml Can: Made in USA by Natural Pigments,Rublesol Odorless Mineral Spirits (16 oz) 473 ml Can,1,10.500,10.500,,"Rublesol Odorless Mineral Spirits (16 oz) 473 ml Can<BR><br> <EOL><b>About Rublesol Pure Odorless Mineral Spirits</b><Br> <EOL>Natural Pigments Rublesol pure odorless mineral spirits (OMS) is an ideal solvent for use with oil and alkyd painting. Rublesol evaporates completely and leaves no residue. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL>Artists use mineral spirits as an alternative to turpentine, because it is less flammable and less toxic. Because of interactions with pigments, painters require a higher grade of mineral spirits, including the absence of residual sulfur. Rublesol odorless mineral spirits have been further refined to remove the more toxic aromatic compounds and is recommended where painters must use an less odorous solvent. <EOL><bR><BR> <EOL><B>Applications:</b><BR> Use to thin oil-based paints for better flow, penetration and leveling. Use to dilute wax and wax pastes. Use as a cleaner to remove wax and oil. Use to clean brushes and for general studio use. <EOL><bR><BR> <EOL><b>Directions:</b><BR> Use Rublesol to dissolve paint, varnish or mediums, then wash off the residue in warm soapy water, and finally rinse in clean water. <EOL><Br><BR> <EOL>Rublesol is less toxic than other brands of OMS, mineral spirits and turpentine, because the aromatic components has been removed. The Permissible Exposure Level (PEL) is 300 parts per million. This means painters can work with Rublesol three times longer than turpentine (100 PEL) without adverse health effects. The higher the PEL, the safer the solvent is to use. <EOL><bR><BR> <EOL><b>Label Information</b><BR> <EOL><B>WARNING!</b><br> Contains Petroleum Distillates. Flammable liquid and vapor. Harmful if swallowed, inhaled or absorbed through skin. Affects central nervous system. Causes irritation to skin, eyes and respiratory tract. Overexposure may result in nausea, headache, con–fusion or instability. <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL><b>PRECAUTIONS:</b><BR> Avoid prolonged contact with skin and breathing vapors. Keep away from eyes. Keep away from heat or flame. Use with adequate venti–lation. KEEP OUT OF REACH OF CHILDREN. <EOL><BR><br> <EOL><b>FIRST AID:</b><br> If swallowed do not induce vomiting, get prompt medical attention. If eye irritation occurs, rinse with water 5-10 minutes. If symptoms occur, move to fresh air. If symptoms persist, see a physician. For further health information contact a local poison control center or call 1-800-445-7067.",,999998,1,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8735,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Vehicles & Mediums/Mineral Spirits,,14.160,,Wednesday 17 November 2010 07:55:23 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,10.500,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
55279,NAP-520-1OMS32,rommel,Rublesol Odorless Mineral Spirits (32 oz) 946 ml Can: Made in USA by Natural Pigments,Rublesol Odorless Mineral Spirits (32 oz) 946 ml Can,2,15.250,15.250,,"Rublesol Odorless Mineral Spirits (32 oz) 946 ml Can<BR><br> <EOL><b>About Rublesol Pure Odorless Mineral Spirits</b><Br> <EOL>Natural Pigments Rublesol pure odorless mineral spirits (OMS) is an ideal solvent for use with oil and alkyd painting. Rublesol evaporates completely and leaves no residue. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL>Artists use mineral spirits as an alternative to turpentine, because it is less flammable and less toxic. Because of interactions with pigments, painters require a higher grade of mineral spirits, including the absence of residual sulfur. Rublesol odorless mineral spirits have been further refined to remove the more toxic aromatic compounds and is recommended where painters must use an less odorous solvent. <EOL><bR><BR> <EOL><B>Applications:</b><BR> Use to thin oil-based paints for better flow, penetration and leveling. Use to dilute wax and wax pastes. Use as a cleaner to remove wax and oil. Use to clean brushes and for general studio use. <EOL><bR><BR> <EOL><b>Directions:</b><BR> Use Rublesol to dissolve paint, varnish or mediums, then wash off the residue in warm soapy water, and finally rinse in clean water. <EOL><Br><BR> <EOL>Rublesol is less toxic than other brands of OMS, mineral spirits and turpentine, because the aromatic components has been removed. The Permissible Exposure Level (PEL) is 300 parts per million. This means painters can work with Rublesol three times longer than turpentine (100 PEL) without adverse health effects. The higher the PEL, the safer the solvent is to use. <EOL><bR><BR> <EOL><b>Label Information</b><BR> <EOL><B>WARNING!</b><br> Contains Petroleum Distillates. Flammable liquid and vapor. Harmful if swallowed, inhaled or absorbed through skin. Affects central nervous system. Causes irritation to skin, eyes and respiratory tract. Overexposure may result in nausea, headache, con–fusion or instability. <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL><b>PRECAUTIONS:</b><BR> Avoid prolonged contact with skin and breathing vapors. Keep away from eyes. Keep away from heat or flame. Use with adequate venti–lation. KEEP OUT OF REACH OF CHILDREN. <EOL><BR><br> <EOL><b>FIRST AID:</b><br> If swallowed do not induce vomiting, get prompt medical attention. If eye irritation occurs, rinse with water 5-10 minutes. If symptoms occur, move to fresh air. If symptoms persist, see a physician. For further health information contact a local poison control center or call 1-800-445-7067.",,1000000,0,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8735,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Vehicles & Mediums/Mineral Spirits,,20.240,,Wednesday 17 November 2010 08:00:32 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,15.250,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
56089,NAP-850-101HP,bhavesh,Rublev Colours 1.5 ml Azurite (Half Pan),Rublev Colours 1.5 ml Azurite (Half Pan),0.010,11.750,11.750,,"Rublev Colours Azurite (Half Pan)<BR><br> <EOL><B>Specifications</b><BR> <EOL><B>Current Names:</b><BR> <EOL>* English: azurite<BR> <EOL>* French: azurite<BR> <EOL>* German: Azurit<BR> <EOL>* Italian: azzurrite<BR> <EOL>* Russian: азурит<BR> <EOL>* Spanish: azurita<BR><BR> <EOL><b>Synonyms:</b><BR> <EOL>* English: Mountain blue<BR> <EOL>* French: bleu de montagne, bleu d'Allemagne<BR> <EOL>* German: Bergblau<Br> <EOL>* Italian: azzurro della magna<BR><BR> <EOL><B>Color Information</b><BR> <EOL>* Single Pigment: Azurite<BR> <EOL>* Binder: 	Gum Arabic<BR><BR> <EOL><B> Pigment Information</b><BR> <EOL>* Color: Blue<BR> <EOL>* Colour Index: 	Pigment Blue 30<BR> <EOL>* Chemical Name: Basic copper carbonate<BR> <EOL>* Chemical Formula: 2CuCO<sub>3</sub>·Cu(OH)<sub>2</sub><BR><BR> <EOL><b>Properties</b><BR> <EOL>* ASTM Lightfastness: Not Rated<BR> <EOL>* Opacity: 	Transparent<BR> <EOL>* Tinting Strength: Medium<BR> <EOL>* Staining: Low<BR> <EOL>* Granulating: High<BR><BR> <EOL><B>About Azurite </b><BR> <EOL>Rublev Colours Azurite is a greenish blue watercolor that is transparent, granulating and non-staining. Azurite is a basic copper carbonate mineral, which is found in many parts of the world in the upper oxidized portions of copper ore deposits. Azurite has been used as a pigment as early as the Fourth Dynasty in Egypt and was the most important blue pigment in European painting during all of the Middle Ages, Renaissance and later until the 18th century. It was largely displaced on the artists' palette by Prussian blue in the 18th century.<BR><BR> <EOL><B>Note:</b> Colors swatches are shown in mass tone, at top, mixed with an equal amount of water and brushed out toward the bottom. All pictures of color swatches in this web site are only approximations of the actual color of the watercolor paint. We have taken every care to match the color in these pictures on calibrated color monitors to the actual color. However, because of the wide variance in color monitors the results you get may vary.",,1000000,0,N,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8459,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments,,15.720,,Monday 22 November 2010 08:55:30 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,11.750,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
56528,NAP-850-903HP,bhavesh,Rublev Colours 1.5 ml Bone Black (Half Pan),Rublev Colours 1.5 ml Bone Black (Half Pan),0.010,4.600,4.600,,"Rublev Colours 1.5 ml Bone Black (Half Pan)<BR><br> <EOL><B>Specifications</b><BR> <EOL><B>Common Names: </b><BR> <EOL>* English: bone black<BR> <EOL>* Dutch: beender zwart<br> <EOL>* French: noir d'os<BR> <EOL>* German: Beinschwarz<BR> <EOL>* Italian: nero di osso<BR> <EOL>* Russian: костяной черный<BR> <EOL>* Spanish: negro de hueso <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL><b>Synonyms:</b><BR> <EOL>* English: ivory black<BR> <EOL>* Dutch: ivoor zwart<BR> <EOL>* French: noir d'ivoire<BR> <EOL>* German: Elfenbeinschwarz<BR> <EOL>* Italian: nero d'avorio<BR> <EOL>* Russian: слоновой кости черный<BR> <EOL>* Spanish: negro de marfil <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL><B>Color Information</b><BR> <EOL>* Single Pigment: Bone Black<BR> <EOL>* Binder: Gum Arabic <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL><B> Pigment Information</b><BR> <EOL>* Color: Black<BR> <EOL>* Colour Index: Pigment Black 9 (77267)<BR> <EOL>* Chemical Name: Hydroxyapatite (calcium phosphate) and carbon<BR> <EOL>* Chemical Formula: Ca<sub>5</sub>(OH)(PO<sub>4</sub>)<sub>3</sub> and C <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL><B>Properties</b><BR> <EOL>* ASTM Lightfastness: I<BR> <EOL>* Opacity: Opaque<BR> <EOL>* Tinting Strength: High<BR> <EOL>* Staining: High<BR> <EOL>* Granulating: None <EOL><br><br> <EOL><B>About Bone Black</b><BR> <EOL>Rublev Colours Bone Black is made from charred animal bone and horn but not from ivory. It is a neutral opaque black that has high tinting strength and is highly staining. The term ivory black is currently applied to a high-grade bone black by other watercolor manufacturers, which is the same grade of bone black we use to make Rublev Colours Bone Black, but we specially make it for watercolors. <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL><b>Note:</b>Colors swatches are shown in mass tone, at top, mixed with an equal amount of water and brushed out toward the bottom. All pictures of color swatches in this web site are only approximations of the actual color of the watercolor paint. We have taken every care to match the color in these pictures on calibrated color monitors to the actual color. However, because of the wide variance in color monitors the results you get may vary.",,1000000,0,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8459,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments,,4.700,,Thursday 25 November 2010 07:04:16 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,3.450,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
