56145,NAP-850-107HP,bhavesh,Rublev Colours 1.5 ml Verditer Blue (Half Pan),Rublev Colours 1.5 ml Verditer Blue (Half Pan),0.010,8.950,8.950,,"Rublev Colours 1.5 ml Verditer Blue (Half Pan)<BR><br> <EOL><B>Specifications</b><BR> <EOL><B>Current Names: </b><BR> <EOL>* English: blue verditer<BR> <EOL>* French: azurite<BR> <EOL>* German: Azurit<BR> <EOL>* Italian: azzurrite<BR> <EOL>* Russian: азурит<BR> <EOL>* Spanish: azurita <BR> <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL><b> Synonyms:</b> <EOL>* English: ashes blue, blue bice, Bremen blue, cendres blue <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL><B>Color Information</b><BR> <EOL>* Single Pigment: Verditer Blue<BR> <EOL>* Binder: Gum Arabic<BR><BR> <EOL> <EOL><B> Pigment Information</b><BR> <EOL>* Color: Blue<BR> <EOL>* Colour Index: Pigment Blue 30<BR> <EOL>* Chemical Name: Basic copper carbonate<BR> <EOL>* Chemical Formula: 2CuCo<sub>3</sub>.Cu(OH)<sub>2</sub> <EOL><b>Properties</b><BR> <EOL>* ASTM Lightfastness: Not Rated<BR> <EOL>* Opacity: Semi-Transparent<BR> <EOL>* Tinting Strength: Moderate<BR> <EOL>* Staining: Moderate<BR> <EOL>* Granulating: 	Low<BR><BR> <EOL><B>About Verditer Blue</b><BR> <EOL>Rublev Colours Verditer Blue is a historic blue watercolor that is semi-transparent and moderately staining. Verditer Blue is artificial basic copper carbonate, which was popular in distemper and water-based paint during the 17th to 19th centuries. Start off with a deep azure and watch how this slightly granulating paint travels quickly in water. Fade the wash to the color of cornflowers for surprising effects. Dilute it to the palest sky blue.<BR><BR> <EOL><B>Note:</b> Colors swatches are shown in mass tone, at top, mixed with an equal amount of water and brushed out toward the bottom. All pictures of color swatches in this web site are only approximations of the actual color of the watercolor paint. We have taken every care to match the color in these pictures on calibrated color monitors to the actual color. However, because of the wide variance in color monitors the results you get may vary. <EOL>",,1000000,0,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8459,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments,,9.120,,Monday 22 November 2010 10:32:17 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,6.710,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
56371,NAP-850-507HP,bhavesh,Rublev Colours 1.5 ml Vermilion (Half Pan),Rublev Colours 1.5 ml Vermilion (Half Pan),0.010,11.750,11.750,,"Rublev Colours 1.5 ml Vermilion (Half Pan)<BR><br> <EOL><B>Specifications</b><BR> <EOL><B>Common Names: </b><BR> <EOL>* English: vermilion<BR> <EOL>* French: vermillon<BR> <EOL>* German: zinnoberrot<BR> <EOL>* Italian: vermiglio<BR> <EOL>* Spanish: bermellón<BR> <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL><B>Color Information</b><BR> <EOL>* Single Pigment: Vermilion<BR> <EOL>* Binder: Gum Arabic <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL><B> Pigment Information</b><BR> <EOL>* Color: Red<BR> <EOL>* Colour Index: Pigment Red 106 (77766)<BR> <EOL>* Chemical Name: Mercuric Sulfide<BR> <EOL>* Chemical Formula: HgS <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL><b>Properties</b><BR> <EOL>* ASTM Lightfastness: II<BR> <EOL>* Opacity: Opaque<BR> <EOL>* Tinting Strength: High<BR> <EOL>* Staining: Average <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL><B>About Vermilion </b><BR> <EOL>Rublev Colours Vermilion Watercolor is an opaque, deep red from vermilion (mercuric sulfide) made by the dry process in China. Vermilion is found on the works of most major watercolorists of the 18th and 19th centuries. It was used by manuscript illuminators since earliest times. <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL><B>Note:</b> Colors swatches are shown in mass tone, at top, mixed with an equal amount of water and brushed out toward the bottom. All pictures of color swatches in this web site are only approximations of the actual color of the watercolor paint. We have taken every care to match the color in these pictures on calibrated color monitors to the actual color. However, because of the wide variance in color monitors the results you get may vary.",,1000000,0,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8801,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Paints/Rublev Colours 1.5 ml Half Pans,,11.880,,Wednesday 24 November 2010 06:06:26 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,8.810,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
