 pigments in medium and calculating binder ratios to make water-based paint. They make adding the right amount of paint binder, such as egg yolk, a no brainer because the right amount of water is already contained in the dispersion, simply add egg yolk.<BR><BR> <EOL><B>Origin and History</b><BR> <EOL>Sartorius red earth is a red earth from Italy based on the mineral hematite. The word hematite comes from the Greek word hema, meaning blood and was given the name ""bloodstone"" in ancient Greece (Theofrastus, c. 325 B.C.), implying that the mineral is blood red in color. Hematite is an important ore of iron and its blood red color in the powdered form lends itself well as a pigment. Red earths, including the mineral hematite, are among the oldest pigments known to humankind and has been used by every major civilization and epoch. <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL><B>Source</b><Br> <EOL>Red ochres are natural earths containing clay tinted by iron oxide and is composed of hematite, and traces of gypsum (calcium sulfate) or calcite (calcium carbonate). Hematite is a mineral composed of iron oxide minerals (á-Fe2O3) that occur as natural earth. Depending upon the content of iron oxide and other minerals, the color of red ochre varies from deep red to brownish red. Red iron oxide minerals, such as hematite, are found around the world and have been used as pigments since prehistory. <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL><B>Permanence and Compatibility</b><BR> <EOL>Red ochres are among the most permanent colors among the artist's palette. It is compatible with all other pigments, and can be used with good results in all mediums. <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL><B>Oil Absorption and Grinding</b><BR> <EOL>Red ochres absorb a medium amount of oil, which may slow the drying of oil paint, but because iron oxide is a drier in oil, it dries fast and forms an excellent film. <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL><B>Toxicity</b><br> <EOL>Red ochres are not considered toxic, but care should be used in handling the dry powder pigment to avoid inhaling the dust.",,1000000,0,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8459,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments,,19.470,,Wednesday 08 December 2010 11:29:23 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,16.160,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
55726,NAP-820-5012,bhavesh,Rublev Colours Red Sartorius Earth 50 ml (1.69 fl oz) Tube,Rublev Colours Red Sartorius Earth 50 ml (1.69 fl oz) Tube,0.040,12.950,12.950,,"Rublev Colours Red Sartorius Earth 50 ml (1.69 fl oz) Tube<BR><br> <EOL><b>Specifications:</b><BR> <EOL><B>Common Names:</b><BR> <EOL>* English: red earth, red ochre<Br> <EOL>* French: terre rouge<Br> <EOL>* German: terrel rot<BR> <EOL>* Italian: terra rosso<Br> <EOL>* Russian: охра красная<BR> <EOL>* Spanish: tierra rojo <BR><BR> <EOL> <EOL><b>Color Information</b><BR> <EOL>* Single Pigment: Natural Iron Oxide<BR> <EOL>* Binder: Linseed Oil<br><br> <EOL> <EOL><B>Pigment Information</b><BR> <EOL>* Color: Orange Red<BR> <EOL>* Colour Index: Pigment Red 102 (77491)<br> <EOL>* Chemical Name: Natural Iron Oxide<BR> <EOL>* Chemical Formula: Fe<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub><BR><BR> <EOL> <EOL><b>Properties</b><BR> <EOL>* ASTM Lightfastness: 	I<br> <EOL>* Opacity: Semi-Transparent<BR> <EOL>* Tinting Strength: High<br> <EOL>* Drying Rate: Above Average<br><br> <EOL> <EOL><b>About Red Sartorius Earth</b><BR> <EOL>Rublev Colours Red Sartorius Earth is a semi- transparent deep orange red with warm undertones, medium grained, high tinting strength and brushes ""long"" in the direction of the brush stroke. Our red earth comes from quarries in Northern Italy.<BR><BR> <EOL> <EOL>Red iron oxide or ferric oxide is the principle coloring agent in red ochres, such as red Sartorius earth. This color is based on the natural mineral hematite associated with varying proportions of other minerals such as clay, chalk and silica. They differ from other ochres in that they do not contain water. In other words, the iron to which they owe their color is iron oxide not hydrated iron oxide.<BR><bR> <EOL> <EOL><B>Note:</b> Colors swatches are shown in mass tone, straight from the tube, on the left, and mixed with an equal amount of Titanium white on the right. All pictures of color swatches in this web site are only approximations of the actual color of the oil paint. We taken every care to match the color in these pictures on calibrated color monitors to the actual color. However, because of the wide variance in color monitors the results you get may vary.<BR>",,1000000,0,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8804,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Paints/Rublev Colours 50 ml (1.7 fl oz) Tubes,,13.090,,Saturday 20 November 2010 04:52:16 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,9.710,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
55407,NAP-810-1105,bhavesh,Rublev Colours Renaissance Palette with Color Tubes,Rublev Colours Renaissance Palette with Color Tubes,0.100,39.950,39.950,,"Rublev Colours Renaissance Palette with Color Tubes<BR><br> <EOL> <EOL><b>About Rublev Colours</b><Br> <EOL>Rublev Renaissance Oil Color Palette gives you an introduction to historical oil colors used since the early Renaissance. These colors can be blended and used interchangeably with other oil colors. <EOL><bR><BR> <EOL>Rublev Oil Colors are based on single, natural mineral and historical pigments that give Old Masters-like painting effects in oil. This series features such exotic colors as natural ultramarine (lapis lazuli), azurite, malachite, among many others. The paint is handcrafted and packed in 50 ml tubes—giving you 33% more color than typical 37 ml tubes. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL>The Renaissance Palette includes three basic colors used by the Old Masters of the high Renaissance in their paintings. The palette includes one full-sized tube each of three colors: Raw Sienna, Yellow Ochre and Red Ochre. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL> <EOL><b>The Renaissance Palette includes one 50 ml tube each of</b><BR><BR> <EOL><a href=""http://www.artistsupplysource.com/product.php?sku=NAP-820-313"" class=""SPItems"" target=""_blank""><img src=""http://www.artistsupplysource.com/image.php?id=62087&type=D"">NAP-820-313</a>, 50 ml Tube<br> <EOL><b>Yellow Ochre Light</b> is a semi-transparent yellow with medium tinting strength, smooth grained that makes bright yellow tints. Rublev yellow ochre is a single pigment color from quarries in northern Italy.<BR><BR> <EOL> <EOL><a href=""http://www.artistsupplysource.com/product.php?sku=NAP-820-503"" class=""SPItems"" target=""_blank""><img src=""http://www.artistsupplysource.com/image.php?id=62088&type=D"">NAP-820-503</a>, 50 ml Tube<br> <EOL><b>Ercolano Red</b> is warm red with good tinting strength. It is semi-opaque, fine grained, goes on smoothly and brushes ""long."" This Rublev red earth is a single pigment color from ochre deposits near Ercolano, Italy.<br><BR> <EOL> <EOL><a href=""http://www.artistsupplysource.com/product.php?sku=NAP-820-606"" class=""SPItems"" target=""_blank""><img src=""http://www.artistsupplysource.com/image.php?id=62089&type=D"">NAP-820-606</a>, 50 ml Tube<br> <EOL><b>Raw Sienna</b> is a semi-transparent brown with good tinting strength, fine grained and brushes ""long"" in the direction of the brush stroke. Our French raw sienna is from the last remaining European company operating the ocher deposits in the heart of the Luberon Mountains, the ocher country.<BR><BR> <EOL> <EOL><B>Note:</b> Colors swatches are shown in mass tone, straight from the tube, on the left, and mixed with an equal amount of Titanium white on the right. All pictures of color swatches in this web site are only approximations of the actual color of the oil paint. We taken every care to match the color in these pictures on calibrated color monitors to the actual color. However, because of the wide variance in color monitors the results you get may vary.",,1000000,0,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8805,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Paints/Rublev Colours Palettes,,35.830,,Thursday 18 November 2010 06:04:32 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,29.960,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
56685,NAP-410-30S,bhavesh2,Rublev Colours Riebeckite 100 g (3.53 oz) Jar,Rublev Colours Riebeckite 100 g (3.53 oz) Jar,0.220,14.500,14.500,,"Rublev Colours Riebeckite 100 g (3.53 oz) Jar<BR><br> <EOL><b>Specifications</b><BR> <EOL><B>Synonyms</b><Br> <EOL>* Abriachanit, Abriachanita, Orthoriebeckit, Orthoriebeckita, Orthoriebeckite, Osanit, Osanita, Osanite, Osannit, Osannita, Osannite, Riebeckit, Riebeckita<BR><BR> <EOL><B>Alternate Names </B><BR> <EOL> <B>English:</b> blue amphibole, blue asbestos, blue Cape asbestos, Crocidolite (variety)<BR><BR> <EOL> <EOL><B>Pigment Information</b><BR> <EOL>* Color: Dark Blue<Br> <EOL>* Colour Index: - <BR> <EOL>* Chemical Name: Sodium Iron Magnesium Silicate Hydroxide<BR> <EOL>* Chemical Formula: Na<sub>2</sub>(Fe, Mg)<sub>3</sub>Fe<sub>2</sub>Si<sub>8</sub>O<sub>22</sub>(OH)<sub>2</sub><BR><BR> <EOL><b> ASTM Lightfastness Rating</b><BR> <EOL>* Acrylic: Not Tested<BR> <EOL>* Oil: Not Tested<BR> <EOL>* Watercolor: Not Tested<BR><BR> <EOL><B>Properties</b><BR> <EOL>* Specific Gravity: 3.2–3.4 (3.2 g/cm<sup>3</sup>)<BR> <EOL>* Hardness:5–6<BR> <EOL>* Refractive Index: a=1.68-1.698, b=1.683-1.7, g=1.685-1.706<BR> <EOL>* Oil Absorption: 20 g oil/100 g pigment <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL><B>About Riebeckite</b><BR> <EOL>Riebeckite is a dark blue mineral from deposits in Krivoi Rog, Ukraine. It has been identified on wall paintings in Late Neolithic and Bronze Age Europe and the eastern Mediterranean. <EOL><Br><BR> <EOL><B>Origin and History of Use</B><BR> <EOL>Riebeckite is a not well-known dark blue mineral although most people have probably seen stones that at one time were composed of riebeckite crystals. The mineral was named after a famous 18th-century German explorer and mineralogist, Emil Riebeck (1853-1885). The mineral was identified on wall paintings on Crete (Knossos, Ayia Triada, Amnissos), mainland Greece (Mycenae) and Cyprus (Akrotiri and Thera), and other works of art since Late Neolithic times (at least the 4th millennium B.C.E.) and the Early Bronze Age, where its was used alone and in combination with Egyptian blue (a synthetic blue pigment). <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL><B>Source</b><BR> <EOL>Riebeckite is a natural silicate mineral belonging to the amphibole group with notable occurrences in Soqotra, South Yemen; Transvaal, South Africa; Schirmeck, Germany; Langesund, Norway; Krivoi Rog, Ukraine; Western Australia; Scotland; Madagascar; and the USA. Rublev riebeckite pigment is made from deposits in Krivoi Rog, Ukraine. Some forms of riebeckite are asbestiform and can be used as asbestos. Asbestos has many industrial uses despite some health risks and is made of different minerals all with a fibrous habit. Riebeckite has a variety called “crocidolite” that is asbestiform in habit, which is the mineral variety of the Rublev pigment.<BR><BR> <EOL><b>Permanence and Compatibility</b><BR> <EOL>Riebeckite is not affected by light and is stable under normal atmosphere. It is resistant to alkalis and hence can be used in fresco and casein.<BR><BR> <EOL><B>Oil Absorption and Grinding</b><BR> <EOL>Riebeckite absorbs a moderate amount of oil (20 g oil per 100 g of pigment). <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL><B>Toxicity</b><BR> <EOL>Riebeckite is an asbestiform mineral and is rated toxic, especially with prolonged exposure by inhalation. It is not considered toxic in contact with the skin. Asbestos is a known carcinogen. Care should be used in handling the dry powder pigment to avoid breathing the dust. A NIOSH-certified dust mask should be worn.",,1000000,0,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8877,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Pigment Powders/Rublev Colours 100 g (3.60 oz.) Pigment Powders,,19.240,,Friday 26 November 2010 09:18:23 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,14.500,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
56684,NAP-410-30B,bhavesh2,Rublev Colours Riebeckite 500 g (17.6 oz) Bag,Rublev Colours Riebeckite 500 g (17.6 oz) Bag,1.100,52.500,52.500,,"Rublev Colours Riebeckite 500 g (17.6 oz) Bag<BR><br> <EOL><b>Specifications</b><BR> <EOL><B>Synonyms</b><Br> <EOL>* Abriachanit, Abriachanita, Orthoriebeckit, Orthoriebeckita, Orthoriebeckite, Osanit, Osanita, Osanite, Osannit, Osannita, Osannite, Riebeckit, Riebeckita<BR><BR> <EOL><B>Alternate Names </B><BR> <EOL>* Abriachanit, Abriachanita, Orthoriebeckit, Orthoriebeckita, Orthoriebeckite, Osanit, Osanita, Osanite, Osannit, Osannita, Osannite, Riebeckit, Riebeckita<BR><BR> <EOL> <EOL><B>Pigment Information</b><BR> <EOL>* Color: Dark Blue<Br> <EOL>* Colour Index: - <BR> <EOL>* Chemical Name: Sodium Iron Magnesium Silicate Hydroxide<BR> <EOL>* Chemical Formula: Na<sub>2</sub>(Fe, Mg)<sub>3</sub>Fe<sub>2</sub>Si<sub>8</sub>O<sub>22</sub>(OH)<sub>2</sub><BR><BR> <EOL><b> ASTM Lightfastness Rating</b><BR> <EOL>* Acrylic: Not Rated<BR> <EOL>* Oil: Not Rated<BR> <EOL>* Watercolor: Not Rated<BR><BR> <EOL><B>Properties</b><BR> <EOL>* Specific Gravity: 3.2–3.4 (3.2 g/cm<sup>3</sup>)<BR> <EOL>* Hardness:5–6<BR> <EOL>* Refractive Index: a=1.68-1.698, b=1.683-1.7, g=1.685-1.706<BR> <EOL>* Oil Absorption: 20 g oil/100 g pigment <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL><B>About Riebeckite</b><BR> <EOL>Riebeckite is a dark blue mineral from deposits in Krivoi Rog, Ukraine. It has been identified on wall paintings in Late Neolithic and Bronze Age Europe and the eastern Mediterranean. <EOL><Br><BR> <EOL><B>Origin and History of Use</B><BR> <EOL>Riebeckite is a not well-known dark blue mineral although most people have probably seen stones that at one time were composed of riebeckite crystals. The mineral was named after a famous 18th-century German explorer and mineralogist, Emil Riebeck (1853-1885). The mineral was identified on wall paintings on Crete (Knossos, Ayia Triada, Amnissos), mainland Greece (Mycenae) and Cyprus (Akrotiri and Thera), and other works of art since Late Neolithic times (at least the 4th millennium B.C.E.) and the Early Bronze Age, where its was used alone and in combination with Egyptian blue (a synthetic blue pigment). <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL><B>Source</b><BR> <EOL>Riebeckite is a natural silicate mineral belonging to the amphibole group with notable occurrences in Soqotra, South Yemen; Transvaal, South Africa; Schirmeck, Germany; Langesund, Norway; Krivoi Rog, Ukraine; Western Australia; Scotland; Madagascar; and the USA. Rublev riebeckite pigment is made from deposits in Krivoi Rog, Ukraine. Some forms of riebeckite are asbestiform and can be used as asbestos. Asbestos has many industrial uses despite some health risks and is made of different minerals all with a fibrous habit. Riebeckite has a variety called “crocidolite” that is asbestiform in habit, which is the mineral variety of the Rublev pigment.<BR><BR> <EOL><b>Permanence and Compatibility</b><BR> <EOL>Riebeckite is not affected by light and is stable under normal atmosphere. It is resistant to alkalis and hence can be used in fresco and casein.<BR><BR> <EOL><B>Oil Absorption and Grinding</b><BR> <EOL>Riebeckite absorbs a moderate amount of oil (20 g oil per 100 g of pigment). <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL><B>Toxicity</b><BR> <EOL>Riebeckite is an asbestiform mineral and is rated toxic, especially with prolonged exposure by inhalation. It is not considered toxic in contact with the skin. Asbestos is a known carcinogen. Care should be used in handling the dry powder pigment to avoid breathing the dust. A NIOSH-certified dust mask should be worn.",,1000000,0,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8878,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Pigment Powders/Rublev Colors 500 g (1.1 lb) Pingment Powders,,67.430,,Friday 26 November 2010 09:07:19 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,52.500,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
55408,NAP-810-1107,bhavesh,Rublev Colours Roger de Piles Flesh Tone Palette with Colors,Rublev Colours Roger de Piles Flesh Tone Palette with Colors,0.100,128.950,128.950,,"Rublev Colours Roger de Piles Flesh Tone Palette<BR><br> <EOL> <EOL><b>About Rublev Colours</b><Br> <EOL>The Rublev Colours Roger de Piles Flesh Tone Palette provides the authentic colors described in the palette of Jombert’s edition of Roger de Piles (pronounced ""peels"") treatise on painting, Les Eleméns de la Peinture Pratique. In his 1684 palette of eight principle colors, Roger de Pies arranged them along the top edge, placing the lightest colors nearest the thumbhole on the wooden palette and arranging tints in rows below. <EOL><bR><BR> <EOL>In 17th century painting practice, pure colors were arranged in a predetermined order on the palette, usually at the top. Next, mixtures or tints of these colors were placed below in columns or rows. The palette was set for specific passages of the painting, such as flesh tones, clothing, drapery or other backgrounds. For the overall painting, Roger described the following limited palette of colors: <EOL><br><BR> <EOL>""In oil painting are usually used eight principal colors: almost all others are derived and are composed of a mixture of these. They are arranged in a range roughly this way. 1. White lead. 2. Yellow ocher. 3. Brown red. 4. Lake. 5. Stil de grain. 6. Green earth. 7. Umber. 8. Bone or ivory black. These are the names of the eight colors and the order in which they are almost always placed on the palette."" <EOL><bR><br> <EOL>Next, he described various color mixtures or tints for painting flesh tones based on the following colors: 1. Lead white. 2. Yellow ocher. 3. Vermilion. 4. Lake. 5. Ultramarine. 6. Bone black. 7. Stil de grain. He then describes the arrangement on the palette: <EOL><br><BR> <EOL>""The eight principal colors occupy places at the top of the palette. The eight colors are, as we have already said, white lead, yellow ocher, brown red, lake, stil de grain, green earth, umber, bone black; you can add carbon black that for some uses is better than another. The shades to paint the flesh tones place below these principal colors and arrange in two rows: those for light values above and those for halftones and shadows below, always observing to put lighter colors nearest the thumbhole. Between these two rows, it is worthwhile putting a little yellow, because you will need it often, and it is more convenient to take it from this place with a brush while painting than to blend with hues from which it was prepared."" <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL>The Roger de Piles Flesh Tones Palette includes the colors used to make the flesh tints described in his painting treatise -- one full-sized tube each of six colors: Lazurite (Lapis Lazuli), for ultramarine; Lemon Ocher, for yellow ocher; Vermilion, Madder Lake, for lake; Italian Sienna, for stil de grain; and Bone Black. <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL>Rublev Colors Artists' Oils are based on single, natural mineral and historical pigments that give old masters-like painting effects in oil. This series features such historical colors as natural ultramarine (lapis lazuli), azurite and lead-tin yellow, among many others. The paint is handcrafted and packed in 50 ml tubes -- giving you 33% more color than typical 37 ml tubes. <EOL><br><BR> <EOL> <EOL><b>Colors included in the Rublev Colours Roger de Piles Flesh Tones Palette</b><br> <EOL><a href=""http://www.artistsupplysource.com/product.php?sku=NAP-820-103"" class=""SPItems"" target=""_blank""><img src=""http://www.artistsupplysource.com/image.php?id=62108&type=D"">NAP-820-103</a>, 50 ml Tube<BR>: <EOL><b>Lazurite (Lapis Lazuli)</b> is a transparent blue from the semi-precious stone, lapis lazuli. It is a medium grained pigment with average tinting strength.<br><bR> <EOL> <EOL><a href=""http://www.artistsupplysource.com/product.php?sku=NAP-820-306"" class=""SPItems"" target=""_blank""><img src=""http://www.artistsupplysource.com/image.php?id=62109&type=D""> <EOL>NAP-820-306</a>, to ml Tube<br> <EOL><b>Lemon Ocher</b> is a transparent yellow that is softly tinting, rich and subtle. It has a subtle, distinct lemon tone that is by no means acid. It works lightly and more transparently than other yellow ochers. In mixtures with red, it is robust without the acid undertones of bright yellows. Our yellow ocher is from quarries in Northern Italy.<BR><BR> <EOL> <EOL><a href=""http://www.artistsupplysource.com/product.php?sku=NAP-820-508"" class=""SPItems"" target=""_blank""><img src=""http://www.artistsupplysource.com/image.php?id=62110&type=D"">NAP-820-508</a>, 50 ml Tube<br> <EOL><b>Vermilion</b> is an opaque bright red with excellent tinting strength and fine grain that makes fleshy pink half tints. Our vermilion is a genuine mercuric sulfide pigment made in China. <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL><a href=""http://www.artistsupplysource.com/product.php?sku=NAP-820-510"" class=""SPItems"" target=""_blank""><img src=""http://www.artistsupplysource.com/image.php?id=62111&type=D"">NAP-820-510</a>, 50 ml Tube<br> <EOL><b>Madder Lake</b> is a transparent bright red with excellent tinting strength and fine grain that makes cold pink half tints.<br><BR> <EOL> <EOL><a href=""http://www.artistsupplysource.com/product.php?sku=NAP-820-611"" class=""SPItems"" target=""_blank""><img src=""http://www.artistsupplysource.com/image.php?id=62112&type=D"">NAP-820-611</a>, 50 ml Tube<BR> <EOL><b>Italian Sienna</b> is a semi- transparent yellow brown with good tinting strength, fine grained and, like most Rublev Colours Artists' Oils, brushes 'long' in the direction of the brush stroke. Our sienna is from quarries in Tuscany, Italy.<br><BR> <EOL> <EOL><a href=""http://www.artistsupplysource.com/product.php?sku=NAP-820-903"" class=""SPItems"" target=""_blank""><img src=""http://www.artistsupplysource.com/image.php?id=62113&type=D"">NAP-820-903</a>, 50 ml Tube<br> <EOL><b>Bone Black</b> is an opaque warm black with good tinting strength and fine grain. It is a high-grade bone black equivalent to what other artists' oils manufacturers name as 'ivory black.' <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL><B>Note:</b> Colors swatches are shown in mass tone, straight from the tube, on the left, and mixed with an equal amount of white on the right. All pictures of color swatches in this web site are only approximations of the actual color of the oil paint. We taken every care to match the color in these pictures on calibrated color monitors to the actual color. However, because of the wide variance in color monitors the results you get may vary.",,999999,1,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8805,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments/Paints/Rublev Colours Palettes,,114.970,,Thursday 18 November 2010 06:09:40 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,96.710,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
57681,NAP-481-131K,bhavesh,Rublev Colours Roman Black (Bulk) 1 Kg (2.2 lb) Bag,Rublev Colours Roman Black (Bulk) 1 Kg (2.2 lb) Bag,2.200,38,38,,"Rublev Colours Roman Black (Bulk) 1 Kg (2.2 lb) Bag<BR><br> <EOL><b>Specifications</b><BR> <EOL><B>Common Names</b><bR> <EOL>* English: black oxide<BR> <EOL>* French: oxyde noir<BR> <EOL>* German: Oxid Schwarzes<BR> <EOL>* Italian: ossido nero<BR> <EOL>* Japanese: 酸化物黒<BR> <EOL>* Portuguese: óxido preto<BR> <EOL>* Spanish: óxido negro<BR><BR> <EOL><B>Alternate Names</b><BR> <EOL>* English: black iron oxide, iron oxide<BR> <EOL>* French: fer oxydé, naturelle noir Indien<BR><BR> <EOL>* Mars black is the name given to the artificial substitute of natural black oxide.<br><BR> <EOL><B>Pigment Information</b><BR> <EOL>* Color: Black<BR> <EOL>* Colour Index: Pigment Black 11 (77499)<BR> <EOL>* Chemical Name: Iron oxide produced from magnetite ore<BR> <EOL>* Chemical Formula: Fe<sub>3</sub>O<sub>4</sub><BR><BR> <EOL><B>ASTM Lightfastness Rating</b><BR> <EOL>* Acrylic: I<BR> <EOL>* Oil: I<BR> <EOL>* Watercolor: I<BR><BR> <EOL><B>Properties</b><BR> <EOL>* Density: 5.15<BR> <EOL>* Hardness: 5.5–6.0<BR> <EOL>* Refractive Index: n=2.42 <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL><b>About Roman Black</b><BR> <EOL>A natural black earth that is a dense, opaque, heavy color that is absolutely permanent. It is comparatively neutral in undertone, wets easily, and is non-greasy, when compared to carbon blacks. It is a useful pigment when these qualities are required.<BR><BR> <EOL><B>Origin and History</b><BR> <EOL>Black oxide is a recent development in modern pigments and was not often encountered in artists' palettes before the 19th century.<BR><BR> <EOL><B>Source</b><BR> <EOL>The source of natural black oxide usually is from an iron ore called magnetite. Magnetite, also known as lodestone, is a ferrous ferric oxide (ferrosic oxide) that is a heavy, black color and opaque. Theoretically, black iron oxide contains slightly more iron metal than red oxide but not nearly as much as yellow oxide. The native mineral may contain varying amounts of manganese, sulfur, clay and silica. <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL><B>Permanence and Compatibility</b><BR> <EOL>Natural black oxide is absolutely permanent colors for all uses on the artist's palette. It is compatible with all other pigments, and can be used with good results in all mediums. <BR><BR> <EOL><B>Oil Absorption and Grinding</b><BR> <EOL>Natural black oxide absorbs a moderately low amount of oil. The oil absorption ratio is 10–15 parts by weight of pigment to 100 parts by weight of linseed oil. If the measurement were grams, it would require 100 grams (by weight) of linseed oil to grind 10 to 15 grams (by weight) of pigment to form a stiff paste. It makes an average drying oil paint, and forms a hard, fairly flexible film. <EOL><BR><BR> <EOL><B>Toxicity</b><BR> <EOL>Natural black oxide is not considered toxic, but care should be used in handling the dry powder pigment to avoid inhaling the dust.",,1000000,0,Y,0.010,N,Y,1,0,0,0,1000,Y,8459,Uncategorized/Natural Pigments,,34.100,,Monday 06 December 2010 07:50:39 AM,0,0,0.600,"2,3,4,6,8,12",0,282,,28.500,0,N,33,Natural Pigments
